/* =====================================================================
   MKT301 study notes (Weeks 1 to 14)
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   NOTES301.weeks[n] holds the notes for teaching week n. They follow the
   lesson objectives in the subject outline (Green, Keegan & Warren,
   Global Marketing, 10th edition, chapters 1 to 17), written in plain
   English for final-year students. Areas that practice questions use
   most get the full treatment; the rest get a line or two.
   Practice material only. Examples are labelled as examples. Anything
   still needing the lecturer's judgement is listed in `review` (checked against the 10th Global Edition).

   Week entry (same fields as MKT101's notes.js topic entry, plus a few):
     wk, title  week number and the outline topic
     big        the big idea in one line
     sum        one-line summary for the week list
     min        "In one minute" points
     lo         what students must be able to do
     ideas      [{h, p (text or list), l (list: text or [term, text]),
                  dia (flow | grid | compare | pyramid | rings), eg (list of examples), tip, more}]
     apply      {h, steps:[[step, text]]} how to use the idea on a case
     terms      [[term, meaning]] key terms (the term shows in bold)
     mistakes   common mistakes
     worked     {q, marks, plan:[...], model:[[tag, paragraph]]}
     check      [[question, answer]] quick checks with hidden answers
     prompts    three study prompts to copy into an AI study helper
   ===================================================================== */

const NOTES301={
 intro:"Short, plain-English notes for all 14 weeks of International Marketing. Each week has the big idea, the key terms, worked examples from global, Maldivian and Asian businesses, a step-by-step way to use the idea on a case, common mistakes, a worked practice question and quick checks.",
 how:[
  ["Start with the big idea","Read the one-line idea and the one-minute summary. Know where the week fits."],
  ["Learn the terms","Each key term is in bold. Say each one in your own words, then flip the cards."],
  ["Use the 'apply it to a case' steps","Every week has a short method. Practise it on the examples, then on the practice cases."],
  ["Test yourself","Answer the quick checks without looking. Then write the worked question on paper before you read the model."]
 ],
 weeks:{},
 review:[]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 10 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[10]={wk:10,title:"Global Marketing Communications Decisions 1: Advertising and Public Relations",
 big:"A global brand must decide how much of its message stays the same everywhere, and how much changes for each country.",
 sum:"Global advertising, standardised versus localised campaigns, the advertising industry, media around the world, and public relations versus publicity.",
 min:[
  "Global advertising means using the same brand name, images and message in many countries.",
  "The key decision is standardise or localise: keep one campaign everywhere, or change the message for each country. Most brands mix the two: one big idea, local details.",
  "Culture, language, religion, the product, advertising laws and the media available all push a brand towards one side or the other.",
  "Big advertising groups (agency holding companies) own many agency brands. Inside an agency, account, creative and media people build a campaign together.",
  "Publicity is media coverage the company does not pay for and cannot control. Public relations (PR) is the work the company does to manage that coverage and build goodwill."
 ],
 lo:"Explain standardised and localised advertising, the factors behind the choice, how agencies and media differ around the world, and how public relations protects a brand abroad.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"What is global advertising?",p:["Global advertising uses the same brand name, images, appeals and message in many country markets.","It saves money, builds one clear brand image, and lets a good idea travel. But one message may not fit every culture."],
   l:[["Global advertising","Messages whose art, copy, headlines, photos and slogans are the same, or very similar, in many countries."],["Big idea","The single creative concept behind a campaign. A good big idea can travel across borders while the details change."]],
   eg:["Example: Red Bull uses the same 'gives you wings' idea and extreme-sports content in almost every market.","Example: Coca-Cola's 'Share a Coke' campaign kept one idea everywhere, but printed local first names on the bottles in each country."]},
  {h:"Standardised or localised advertising",p:"This is the most important decision in global advertising. Think of it as a scale, not a yes-or-no choice.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Two ends of the scale",cols:[["Standardised (extension)",["Same message and creative everywhere","Lower cost, one brand image","Fits universal products and global segments","Risk: message may not fit local culture"]],["Localised (adaptation)",["Language, images or appeal change by country","Higher cost, more local relevance","Fits culture-bound products such as food and beauty","Risk: brand image can become inconsistent"]]]},
   l:[["Standardised advertising","The same message and creative in every country. Only the language may change."],["Localised advertising","The message, images or appeal are changed to suit each country's culture, laws or media."],["Pattern advertising","The middle way: one overall theme and look, with local copy, faces and details."]],
   eg:["Example: Dove keeps its 'real beauty' theme worldwide, but uses local women and local beauty issues in each country. That is pattern advertising.","Example: A Sri Lankan tea brand shows a calm morning cup in Europe, but a family sharing tea at a festival in South Asia. Same product, localised appeal."],
   tip:"Do not just define the two types. Always say which one fits the business in the question, and why."},
  {h:"What decides standardise or localise?",p:"Look for these factors in any case. Each one pushes the decision one way.",
   l:[["Culture, language and religion","Where values, humour or religion differ a lot, the message usually needs to change."],["Type of product","Universal products (phones, airlines, luxury goods) can standardise. Culture-bound products (food, drinks, beauty, clothing) often need to localise."],["Advertising laws","Some countries ban or limit ads for alcohol, tobacco, medicine, or ads aimed at children. Comparative ads are banned in some places."],["Media available","If TV is weak but mobile is strong, the media plan must change even if the message does not."],["Global target segment","If the target is the same everywhere (for example, global business travellers or young gamers), one message can work."],["Cost and brand control","One campaign is cheaper and keeps the brand consistent."]],
   eg:["Example: An airline such as Emirates can use one global campaign about comfort and service, because travellers want the same benefits everywhere.","Example: A noodle brand like Maggi changes flavours and ad stories in each Asian country, because food tastes are local."]},
  {h:"The advertising industry and agency people",p:["Most global advertising is made by agencies. A few huge agency holding companies own many agency brands around the world.","An agency brand is the name a client works with. The holding company owns it, with other brands, and shares money and data across them."],
   l:[["Agency holding company","A group that owns several agency brands, for example WPP, Omnicom or Publicis Groupe."],["Agency brand","An individual agency the client deals with, such as Ogilvy (owned by WPP)."],["Account services","The account manager links the client and the agency, and keeps the campaign on brief."],["Creative team","Copywriters and art directors turn the strategy into words and pictures."],["Media planning and buying","Planners choose which media reach the target; buyers buy the space and time."]],
   more:"The world's biggest advertisers are mostly large consumer-goods, car, tech and online retail companies. You do not need exact rankings; know that big global firms spend billions each year.",
   eg:["Example: A global drinks company may hire one agency brand from a holding company to run its campaign in 40 countries, using the holding company's local offices for media buying."]},
  {h:"Media around the world",p:"Media availability varies by country. The best message fails if it is in the wrong media.",
   l:[["Mobile first","In much of Asia and Africa, many people reach the internet mainly through phones, so short video and social media matter more."],["Government limits","Some governments control TV or limit foreign ads, or restrict ad time."],["Local media habits","Radio still matters in rural areas. Outdoor and cinema ads are strong in some cities."]],
   eg:["Example: In the Maldives, many brands reach young people through Instagram and TikTok, and reach families through local TV and Viber groups, rather than newspapers.","Example: In India and Indonesia, short video ads on phones reach far more young buyers than print."]},
  {h:"Public relations and publicity",p:"Customers trust news and word of mouth more than ads. That is why PR matters, especially in a foreign market where the brand is new.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Publicity and public relations",cols:[["Publicity",["Media coverage about the company","Not paid for","The company cannot control it","Can be good or bad"]],["Public relations",["Planned work by the company","Builds goodwill and trust","Manages publicity, especially in a crisis","Tools: press releases, events, sponsorship, social media, community work"]]]},
   l:[["Publicity","Communication about a company or product that the company does not pay for and does not control."],["Public relations (PR)","The company's planned work to build goodwill with the public, media and government, and to manage publicity."],["Crisis management","How PR responds when something goes wrong: act fast, tell the truth, show care, fix the problem, and update people."]],
   eg:["Example: In 2015, food safety officials in India said Maggi noodles had too much lead. Nestlé withdrew the product. It later returned with safety tests, clear messages and celebrity support. This shows bad publicity, then PR to rebuild trust.","Example: When Samsung's Galaxy Note 7 batteries caught fire in 2016, Samsung recalled every phone worldwide and later explained its new safety checks. A fast, global recall protected the brand.","Example: A Maldivian resort that cleans reefs with local schools and shares the story with travel media is using PR to win good publicity."],
   tip:"Publicity is the coverage. PR is the work. If a question asks for the difference, say who controls it and who pays."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Name the choice","Standardise, localise, or a mix (pattern). Say which."],["Find two factors in the case","Culture, product type, laws, media, segment, cost. Quote facts."],["Link factor to decision","'Because ___, the brand should ___.'"],["Give one example of the message","Show what the ad would say or show in that country."],["For PR, plan the response","What happened, what the company does first, who it talks to, how it rebuilds trust."]]},
 terms:[["Global advertising","The same or very similar ad messages, images and slogans used in many countries."],["Standardised advertising","One message and creative for every country."],["Localised advertising","Ads changed to suit each country's culture, laws or media."],["Pattern advertising","One theme across countries, with local details."],["Agency holding company","A group that owns several advertising agency brands."],["Agency brand","An individual agency inside a holding company."],["Media availability","Which media exist and reach people in a country, and what rules apply to them."],["Publicity","Unpaid, uncontrolled media coverage about a company."],["Public relations","Planned activity to build goodwill and manage publicity."]],
 mistakes:["Saying 'standardised means the same language'. Standardised means the same message and creative; the language can still be translated.","Giving a factor without linking it to the decision ('culture is important' gets little credit).","Mixing up publicity and PR.","Using an example without saying which type of advertising it shows."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Thai skincare brand plans to advertise its sunscreen in Japan, Australia and the Gulf states. Recommend how far it should adapt one campaign for these markets, and show how the ads in Japan and the Gulf would look different.",
  plan:["Define standardised and localised advertising.","Factor 1: culture and religion (for example, modest images in the Gulf states).","Factor 2: product and customer needs (skin types, sun strength, beauty ideals).","Recommend pattern advertising: one theme, local details.","Example: what the Japan ad and the Gulf ad would show."],
  model:[["Define","Standardised advertising uses the same message and creative in every country. Localised advertising changes the message, images or appeal to suit each country."],["Factor 1","Culture and religion differ. In the Gulf states, ads must show modest clothing, so beach images used in Australia would not fit."],["Factor 2","Customer needs differ. Australians want strong sun protection for outdoor life. Many Japanese buyers want light, non-greasy sunscreen that keeps skin bright."],["Recommend","The brand should use pattern advertising: one theme, such as 'protection that feels like nothing', with local models, images and benefits."],["Example","The Japan ad could show an office worker applying a light cream before a commute. The Gulf ad could show a family day in a park, with modest clothing."]]},
 check:[["What is the difference between publicity and public relations?","Publicity is unpaid media coverage the company cannot control. PR is the company's planned work to build goodwill and manage that coverage."],["Name three factors that push a brand to localise its ads.","Any three: culture, language, religion, culture-bound product, advertising laws, media habits."],["What is pattern advertising?","One overall theme and look in every country, with local copy, faces and details."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain standardised, localised and pattern advertising in simple English, with one Asian brand example of each. Then give me a short case and ask me to choose one and justify it.","Explain the difference between publicity and public relations in simple English. Then give me a short crisis story about a food brand in a foreign country and ask me to plan the PR response in five steps.","Act as my tutor. Ask me five quick questions on global advertising: agency holding companies, agency roles and media availability around the world. Correct my answers briefly."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 11 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[11]={wk:11,title:"Global Marketing Communications Decisions 2: Sales Promotion, Personal Selling and Special Forms",
 big:"Beyond advertising, a global brand uses short-term offers, people and special media to move customers from interest to action.",
 sum:"Sales promotion tools, the strategic/consultative personal selling model, choosing the nationality of the sales force, direct marketing, and support media, sponsorship and product placement.",
 min:[
  "Sales promotion is any short-term offer that gives customers or the trade a reason to buy now: samples, coupons, discounts, contests, loyalty points.",
  "Promotions must fit local laws and habits. Some countries limit discounts, contests or free gifts.",
  "Personal selling is face-to-face (or video) communication with a buyer. It matters most for expensive, technical or business products.",
  "The strategic/consultative selling model has five strategic steps and a six-step presentation plan.",
  "Companies choose expatriate, host-country or third-country salespeople, depending on the market, the product and the customer.",
  "Direct marketing, sponsorship, product placement and support media complete the promotion mix. Each tool should reach a clear customer group."
 ],
 lo:"Choose suitable sales promotion tools for a foreign market, explain the personal selling model and sales force nationality, and show how direct marketing, sponsorship and product placement fit the promotion mix.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Sales promotion",p:["Sales promotion is any paid, short-term communication that adds value to a product to make people buy now. It can target consumers or the trade (distributors and retailers).","It works well when a brand is new in a country: it lowers the risk of trying it."],
   l:[["Sampling","Giving a small free amount so people can try the product."],["Coupons and price deals","Money off, buy-one-get-one, or bundle offers."],["Contests and sweepstakes","Chances to win prizes. Some countries restrict games of chance."],["Loyalty programmes","Points or rewards for repeat buying."],["Trade promotions","Extra margin, free stock or display support for retailers so they stock and show the product."]],
   eg:["Example: When a new energy drink enters Sri Lanka, the brand gives free cans at universities and gyms to get trial from young adults.","Example: A Maldivian telecom company gives extra data to customers who top up during Ramadan nights.","Example: A shampoo brand gives supermarkets in Indonesia a free shelf display and a discount if they order 20 cartons."],
   tip:"Match each tool to a goal: trial (samples), repeat buying (loyalty points), or getting shelf space (trade promotion)."},
  {h:"Sales promotion abroad: what to watch",l:[["Laws","Some countries cap discount levels, ban some contests, or limit free gifts with purchase."],["Culture and religion","Prizes and timing must suit local values, for example no alcohol prizes in Muslim countries."],["Retail structure","Where shops are small and independent, coupons are harder to manage than instant discounts."],["Brand image","Too many deals can cheapen a premium brand."]],
   eg:["Example: A premium chocolate brand avoids deep discounts in Japan, because buyers link low prices with low quality. It uses limited-edition gift boxes instead."]},
  {h:"Personal selling and the strategic/consultative model",p:"Personal selling is two-way, person-to-person communication with a buyer. It is costly, so it is used where products are expensive, complex or sold business-to-business. The model has two parts: a strategy, then the presentation.",
   dia:{type:"flow",title:"Strategic steps (before meeting the buyer)",steps:[["Personal selling philosophy","Believe in the marketing concept: solve the customer's problem."],["Relationship strategy","Plan to build trust and long-term relationships."],["Product strategy","Know the product well and how it creates value for this buyer."],["Customer strategy","Understand the buyer's needs, buying process and culture."],["Presentation strategy","Set goals for each call and plan how to deliver the presentation."]]},
   more:"The presentation plan then follows six steps: approach (make contact and build rapport), presentation (find needs and show benefits), demonstration (show it working), negotiation (handle concerns, agree terms), close (ask for the order), and servicing (follow up after the sale).",
   eg:["Example: A Japanese company selling water desalination units to resort islands first studies each resort's water use (customer strategy), then demonstrates a small unit on site, agrees a service contract (negotiation), and visits every six months (servicing)."],
   tip:"In high-context cultures, the approach and relationship steps take longer. Do not rush to the close."},
  {h:"Sales force nationality",p:"Who should sell for the company abroad? There are three choices.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Three choices of salesperson",cols:[["Expatriate",["From the home country","Knows the product and company well","Costly; may not know local culture"]],["Host-country national",["From the local market","Knows language, culture and buyers","Less known to head office; may need product training"]],["Third-country national",["From another country","Often regional experience and languages","Can be a good middle option"]]]},
   more:"The textbook names three contingency factors. 1) Management orientation: ethnocentric firms tend to send expatriates everywhere. 2) How technical the product is: complex products sold in developed countries often need expatriate experts; simpler products can use host-country staff. 3) The country's stage of development: in less-developed countries, host-country staff are usually the better choice. Cost, language and buyers' preferences also matter.",
   l:[["Management orientation","Ethnocentric firms prefer expatriates; polycentric and geocentric firms use more local and third-country staff."],["Technical level of the product","A complex, technical product may need home-country experts, at least at first."],["Stage of economic development","In less-developed markets, host-country nationals usually work best."],["Cost and customers","Expatriates cost much more; buyers who prefer to deal with people of their own culture favour host-country staff."]],
   eg:["Example: A German machinery maker sends engineers from Germany to win its first buyers in Vietnam, then hires and trains Vietnamese sales staff.","Example: A Singapore-based regional manager from India leads sales for a software firm across South Asia: a third-country national."]},
  {h:"Direct marketing",p:"Direct marketing communicates straight with chosen customers to get a response, such as an order or a visit. It can be measured easily.",
   l:[["Direct mail and email","Personal letters or emails to chosen customers."],["Catalogues","Printed or online product books."],["Infomercials and TV shopping","Long TV ads or shopping channels that sell directly."],["Online and mobile","Apps, messaging, text offers and social media ads with a 'buy' button."]],
   eg:["Example: A Maldivian airline emails past passengers a discount on a route they have flown before.","Example: A Korean beauty brand sells to Malaysian buyers through its own app with a first-order discount."],
   tip:"Direct marketing must follow local data and privacy laws, and some customers dislike unwanted messages."},
  {h:"Support media, sponsorship and product placement",l:[["Support media","Outdoor (billboards, transit ads) and other media that remind people about the brand."],["Sponsorship","Paying to link the brand with an event, team or cause the target audience cares about."],["Product placement","Paying for the brand to appear inside a film, TV show, music video or game."]],
   eg:["Example: Emirates sponsors football clubs and tennis tournaments to reach fans around the world.","Example: Korean drama series show phones, cars and cosmetics on screen, which raises demand across Asia.","Example: A Maldivian bank sponsors a national school sports event to build goodwill with families."],
   tip:"Always say who the tool reaches. 'Sponsorship of a football club reaches young male fans in many countries' is better than 'sponsorship increases awareness'."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Name the customer group","Who are we trying to reach? Use the case's segments."],["Choose the tool","Sampling, discount, loyalty, trade deal, personal selling, sponsorship, placement or direct marketing."],["Say why it fits","Link the tool to that group's habits and to the brand's image."],["Check local limits","Laws, culture, religion, media habits."],["Say how you will measure it","Trial numbers, repeat purchase, leads, sales."]]},
 terms:[["Sales promotion","A short-term offer that gives a reason to buy now."],["Trade promotion","Incentives for retailers or distributors to stock and display a product."],["Personal selling","Person-to-person communication to persuade a buyer."],["Strategic/consultative selling model","A selling approach with five strategic steps and a six-step presentation plan."],["Expatriate","An employee working outside their home country."],["Host-country national","A local employee in the foreign market."],["Third-country national","An employee from neither the home country nor the host market."],["Direct marketing","Communication straight to chosen customers to get a measurable response."],["Sponsorship","Paying to link a brand with an event, team or cause."],["Product placement","Paying for a brand to appear in films, shows or games."]],
 mistakes:["Listing promotion tools without saying which customer group each one reaches.","Forgetting that some promotions are illegal or unsuitable in some countries.","Mixing up the five strategic steps with the six presentation steps.","Choosing expatriate staff without explaining the higher cost."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Malaysian halal cosmetics brand is launching in Pakistan and the United Kingdom. Plan the launch promotion for each country: choose one sales promotion and one other promotion tool, and explain what each would achieve.",
  plan:["Set the launch goal: get people to try the product and trust the halal claim.","Tool 1 with reasons: sampling through beauty stores and universities.","Tool 2 with reasons: sponsorship or social media influencers.","Check one local limit (privacy, culture).","Say how success would be measured."],
  model:[["Tool 1: sampling","The brand should give free samples in beauty stores and at women's universities in Lahore and Karachi. Young urban women like to try new products before buying, so a sample lowers the risk of switching brands."],["Tool 2: influencers and events","In the UK, the brand could sponsor modest-fashion events and work with British Muslim beauty creators online. This reaches young Muslim shoppers who look for halal beauty products they can trust."],["Fit and limits","Both tools build trial without heavy discounting, so the brand keeps a quality image. Promotions must follow UK advertising rules on honest claims."],["Measure","Count samples given, app sign-ups and repeat purchases in the first three months."]]},
 check:[["Name the six steps of the selling presentation.","Approach, presentation, demonstration, negotiation, close, servicing."],["Give one reason to hire host-country salespeople.","They know the language, culture and buyers, and cost less than expatriates."],["What is product placement?","Paying for a brand to appear inside a film, TV show, music video or game."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain the strategic/consultative selling model in simple English, using a company selling equipment to resorts as the example.","Give me three short stories about companies entering new countries. For each, ask me which sales promotion tool fits best and why. Correct my answers.","Explain expatriate, host-country and third-country salespeople in simple English. Then give me a case and ask me to choose one and justify it."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 1 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[1]={wk:1,title:"Introduction to Global Marketing",
 big:"Global marketing is about deciding where to grow, how much to adapt, and what mindset guides those choices.",
 sum:"The growth matrix, global marketing strategy versus single-country strategy, leverage, and the four management orientations (EPRG).",
 min:[
  "A company can grow in four ways, shown in the product/market growth matrix: market penetration, market development, product development and diversification.",
  "A global marketing strategy (GMS) plans the marketing mix across many countries, choosing what to standardise and what to adapt.",
  "Companies that work in many countries gain leverage: they can reuse experience, scale, resources and good ideas.",
  "Managers' mindset shapes strategy. The EPRG framework names four orientations: ethnocentric, polycentric, regiocentric and geocentric.",
  "Firms compete by offering more value: better benefits, a better price, or both."
 ],
 lo:"Use the growth matrix, compare single-country and global strategies, explain leverage, and identify a company's management orientation with evidence.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"The product/market growth matrix",p:"The matrix shows four ways to grow, based on whether the product and the market are existing or new.",
   dia:{type:"grid",title:"Product/market growth matrix",yl:"Market",y:["Existing","New"],xl:"Product",x:["Existing","New"],cells:[["Market penetration","Sell more of the same product in the same market."],["Product development","Sell a new product in the same market."],["Market development","Sell the same product in a new market (often a new country)."],["Diversification","Sell a new product in a new market. Highest risk."]]},
   eg:["Example (market development): A Maldivian swimwear label that sells online in the Maldives starts selling the same designs to buyers in Australia.","Example (product development): A Maldivian guesthouse adds its own snorkelling and sandbank trips for its existing guests.","Example (diversification): Grab began with ride-hailing in Malaysia, then added food delivery and digital payments across Southeast Asia.","Example (penetration): A soft-drink brand runs price offers and new pack sizes to sell more in a market it already serves."],
   tip:"Global expansion is usually market development: same product, new country. Say so, and say which country."},
  {h:"Single-country versus global marketing strategy",p:["A single-country strategy plans the marketing mix for one market.","A global marketing strategy (GMS) looks at the world, or a region, as one set of markets. It decides which markets to enter, and how much of the marketing mix to standardise or adapt."],
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Two kinds of strategy",cols:[["Single-country strategy",["One market","Marketing mix built for local needs","Little sharing between countries"]],["Global marketing strategy",["Many countries planned together","Some parts standardised, some adapted","Shares ideas, scale and brand across countries"]]]},
   l:[["Standardisation","Using the same product, brand, price, promotion or process across countries."],["Adaptation","Changing parts of the marketing mix for a country."],["Global market participation","How many countries, and which ones, the company sells in."]],
   tip:"A global strategy does not mean 'everything the same everywhere'. It means planning the whole world together, then standardising where possible and adapting where needed."},
  {h:"Leverage",p:"Leverage is the advantage a company gets because it works in many countries. Experience and resources from one country help in another.",
   l:[["Experience transfers","A product, campaign or process that works in one country is reused in another."],["Scale economies","Bigger total volume lowers cost per unit in production, buying and marketing."],["Resource utilisation","The company can move money, people and supplies to where they create most value."],["Global strategy","A global view of the business lets it spot opportunities and risks earlier."]],
   eg:["Example: Unilever tests a new shampoo format in one Asian market, then rolls the winning idea out to others with little extra cost (experience transfer).","Example: Toyota builds several car models on the same platform in many factories, which lowers cost per car (scale economies)."]},
  {h:"Management orientation: the EPRG framework",p:"EPRG describes how managers see the world. Each orientation leads to different marketing decisions. Find the evidence in a case, then name it.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Four orientations",cols:[["Ethnocentric",["Home country is best","Same product and approach everywhere","Overseas = extra sales"]],["Polycentric",["Every country is unique","Each country run separately","Local managers decide almost everything"]],["Regiocentric",["A region is the unit","One strategy for a region (for example, ASEAN or the Gulf)","Some local changes within the region"]],["Geocentric",["The world is one market","Global strategy with local response where needed","Best ideas from anywhere"]]]},
   l:[["Ethnocentric","Managers believe home-country products and methods are best and use them abroad without change."],["Polycentric","Managers believe each country is different and needs its own separate strategy."],["Regiocentric","Managers treat a region as one market and build a regional strategy."],["Geocentric","Managers see the world as one market and build a global strategy that still responds to local needs."]],
   eg:["Example (ethnocentric): A small exporter sells exactly the same product, label and price list overseas as at home.","Example (polycentric): A hotel group lets each country manager choose its own brand name, design and prices.","Example (regiocentric): A food brand builds one plan for all of South Asia, with one brand and one factory, and small flavour changes per country.","Example (geocentric): Toyota plans products, factories and suppliers worldwide, while adapting models for local roads and tastes."],
   tip:"Always name the orientation AND quote the evidence: 'same recipes everywhere' points to ethnocentric; 'separate business in each country' points to polycentric; 'one plan for the region' points to regiocentric."},
  {h:"Competitive advantage and value",p:"Companies win by giving customers more value than competitors. Value is the benefits customers get compared with the price they pay.",
   l:[["Value","Benefits divided by price. Raise the benefits, lower the price, or both."],["Competitive advantage","Offering better value than rivals in a way that lasts."]],
   eg:["Example: A budget airline in Asia offers low fares (lower price), while a full-service airline offers more comfort and service (more benefits). Both offer value to different customers."]}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Spot the growth type","Same or new product? Same or new market? Name the box."],["Find the mindset","Quote what managers say or plan: same everywhere, separate countries, one region, or one world."],["Name the orientation","Ethnocentric, polycentric, regiocentric or geocentric, with the quote as proof."],["Judge it","Is this orientation a good fit for this company and these markets? Why?"],["Suggest a change","For example, 'move from ethnocentric to regiocentric so it can adapt to halal rules'."]]},
 terms:[["Market penetration","Selling more of existing products in existing markets."],["Market development","Selling existing products in new markets."],["Product development","Selling new products in existing markets."],["Diversification","Selling new products in new markets."],["Global marketing strategy (GMS)","A plan that treats many countries together and chooses what to standardise and adapt."],["Leverage","The advantages a company gains from operating in many countries."],["Ethnocentric","Home-country methods are best; use them abroad."],["Polycentric","Each country is unique; run each separately."],["Regiocentric","The region is the market; one regional strategy."],["Geocentric","The world is one market, with local response where needed."]],
 mistakes:["Naming an orientation without evidence from the case.","Mixing up regiocentric (one region) and geocentric (the whole world).","Calling every overseas sale 'diversification'. Selling the same product in a new country is market development.","Thinking global strategy means no adaptation at all."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Sri Lankan tea company sells its teas in the Middle East through local distributors. Each distributor chooses its own packaging, prices and adverts, and head office does not get involved. What does this tell you about how head office sees foreign markets? What could go wrong, and what should it change?",
  plan:["Name the orientation: polycentric.","Evidence: each distributor decides packaging, prices and ads; no central role.","Risk: inconsistent brand and prices; no shared learning.","Suggest regiocentric: one Middle East plan, with local changes.","Explain why it fits."],
  model:[["Identify","The company shows a polycentric orientation, because each distributor runs its market separately and chooses its own packaging, prices and adverts."],["Risk","The brand may look and cost different in every country. Customers who travel in the Gulf may be confused, and traders could buy cheap stock in one country and sell it in another. Good ideas from one market are not shared."],["Suggest","A regiocentric orientation would fit better. The company could build one Middle East plan with one brand look and one price position, while each country adjusts pack sizes or language."],["Why","The Gulf countries share language, religion and many tastes, so a regional plan keeps the brand strong and still allows small local changes."]]},
 check:[["Same product, new country: which growth strategy is this?","Market development."],["Which orientation treats a region, such as ASEAN, as one market?","Regiocentric."],["Name two types of leverage.","Any two: experience transfers, scale economies, resource utilisation, global strategy."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain the EPRG framework in simple English with one Asian company example for each orientation. Then give me three short company stories and ask me to name the orientation with evidence.","Explain the product/market growth matrix in simple English, using Maldivian businesses as examples. Then ask me to place four new examples in the right box.","Explain leverage in global marketing with simple examples. Then quiz me with three short questions."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 2 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[2]={wk:2,title:"The International Business Environment",
 big:"Every country differs in its economy, trade rules, culture, politics and laws, and those differences decide what a brand must change.",
 sum:"The world economy, economic systems and stages of development, the WTO and trade blocs, culture (high and low context), politics and law.",
 min:[
  "The world economy has become far more connected: trade, investment and technology now cross borders quickly.",
  "Countries have different economic systems and income levels. The World Bank groups them as low, lower-middle, upper-middle and high income.",
  "The WTO sets trade rules between members. Regional trade blocs (such as ASEAN, SAFTA, the GCC and the EU) cut barriers between members.",
  "Culture shapes what people buy and how they communicate. High-context cultures rely on relationships and unspoken meaning; low-context cultures rely on clear words and contracts.",
  "Political risk and legal systems decide what a company may do, and how safe its brand and property are.",
  "In a case, the key skill is to pick two or three differences between countries and say what each one forces the company to change."
 ],
 lo:"Explain how economic, trade, cultural, political and legal differences between countries affect global marketing decisions, using evidence from a case.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"The changing world economy",p:"In the last hundred years, the world economy has changed in big ways. You only need the main shifts.",
   l:["Capital movements (money for investment) are now much bigger than trade in goods.","Production has moved away from the link with employment: factories make more with fewer workers.","The world economy, not the national economy, is now the main unit that matters for many firms.","The fight between capitalism and socialism has largely ended; most countries now use some form of market economy.","Growth of e-commerce lets even small firms sell across borders."]},
  {h:"Economic systems and stages of development",dia:{type:"flow",title:"World Bank income groups",steps:[["Low income","Mostly farming; small consumer markets."],["Lower-middle income","Growing factories and cities, for example parts of South Asia."],["Upper-middle income","Fast growth, rising middle class, for example Malaysia and Thailand."],["High income","Rich, service-based economies, for example Singapore and the Gulf states."]]},
   l:[["Market capitalism","Private ownership; prices set by supply and demand."],["Centrally planned socialism","The state owns resources and plans output."],["Centrally planned capitalism / market socialism","Mixes: for example, strong state control with private business."],["Emerging markets","Countries growing quickly with rising incomes, such as India, Indonesia and Vietnam."]],
   eg:["Example: A phone maker sells cheaper models with long battery life in lower-middle-income markets, and premium models in high-income markets."],
   tip:"Income level is one of the strongest reasons for different prices and pack sizes between countries."},
  {h:"The WTO and regional economic integration",p:["The World Trade Organization (WTO) sets rules for trade between members and settles trade disputes.","Countries also join regional trade agreements. The deeper the integration, the more rules they share."],
   dia:{type:"flow",title:"Levels of economic integration",steps:[["Free trade area","No tariffs between members; each keeps its own tariffs for outsiders. Example: ASEAN Free Trade Area."],["Customs union","Free trade plus a common external tariff. Example: the GCC customs union."],["Common market","Customs union plus free movement of labour and capital."],["Economic union","Common market plus shared economic policy, and often one currency. Example: the euro area of the EU."]]},
   eg:["Example: A Thai food company can export to Malaysia and Vietnam with low or no tariffs because they are all ASEAN members.","Example: The Maldives is a member of SAFTA (South Asian Free Trade Area) with India, Sri Lanka and others."],
   tip:"If a case mentions a trade bloc, say what it makes easier: lower tariffs, simpler customs, or a single regional factory."},
  {h:"Culture: what matters for marketing",p:"Culture is a society's shared ways of living: values, beliefs, religion, language, food, family roles and customs. It affects products, communication and how business is done.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"High-context and low-context cultures",cols:[["High-context",["Meaning comes from relationships and the situation","Trust built over time; personal visits matter","Indirect communication","Examples: Japan, Thailand, Arab countries, the Maldives"]],["Low-context",["Meaning is in the words","Contracts and facts matter most","Direct communication","Examples: Germany, the USA, Australia"]]]},
   l:[["Religion","Shapes food rules (halal, vegetarian), holidays, dress and ad images."],["Language","Brand names, labels and slogans may need changes."],["Values and attitudes","For example, attitudes to time, family, status and saving."],["Food and taste","Often the most local part of culture; food brands usually adapt."]],
   eg:["Example: KFC in Japan built a Christmas tradition of eating fried chicken, linking the brand to a local custom.","Example: Food sold in the Maldives, Malaysia, Pakistan and the Gulf must be halal-certified.","Example: Business partners in Thailand or the Gulf expect relationship-building meetings before signing; in Australia a clear written offer may be enough."]},
  {h:"The political environment",l:[["Political risk","The risk that government action or instability harms a business, for example sudden rule changes, unrest or seizure of assets."],["Taxes","Tax rules differ and can change the profit from a market."],["Expropriation","When a government takes over a foreign company's property, sometimes without fair payment."],["Sovereignty","A country's right to set its own rules, which can conflict with global companies' plans."]],
   eg:["Example: A sudden import ban on a product category can stop an exporter's sales overnight."]},
  {h:"The legal environment",p:"There is no single world law. International law covers agreements between countries; each country also has its own system.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Main legal systems",cols:[["Common law",["Based on court decisions and custom","Examples: UK, India, Singapore"]],["Civil law",["Based on written codes","Examples: France, Germany, Japan, Thailand"]],["Islamic law (Sharia)",["Based on religious principles","Affects contracts, finance and food","Examples: Saudi Arabia, Iran; many Muslim countries, including the Maldives, mix Islamic law with other legal traditions"]]]},
   l:[["Jurisdiction","Which country's courts decide a dispute."],["Intellectual property","Patents, trademarks and copyrights. Register your brand in each country before entering."],["Competition (antitrust) law","Rules against price-fixing and abuse of market power."],["Licensing and trade secrets","Contracts protect know-how shared with partners."],["Bribery and corruption","Laws such as the US Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and the UK Bribery Act punish bribes paid abroad."]],
   eg:["Example: A brand that enters a country without registering its trademark may find a local firm has already registered the name."],
   tip:"For legal questions, give the issue, the risk, and one way to protect the business."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["List the countries","Write the markets in the case side by side."],["Find two or three differences","Income, religion and halal, taste, language, laws, trade bloc, context of culture."],["Say what each difference forces","'Because Malaysia is Muslim-majority, the product must be halal-certified.'"],["Say what can stay the same","The brand name or core product often stays; details change."],["Conclude","Small local changes, big changes, or a regional approach?"]]},
 terms:[["Emerging market","A country with fast growth and rising incomes."],["WTO","The World Trade Organization, which sets global trade rules and settles disputes."],["Free trade area","Members remove tariffs between themselves."],["Customs union","A free trade area with a common external tariff."],["Common market","A customs union with free movement of labour and capital."],["Economic union","A common market with shared economic policy, often a single currency."],["High-context culture","Meaning comes from relationships and situation, not just words."],["Low-context culture","Meaning is stated clearly in words and contracts."],["Political risk","The risk that government action or instability harms a business."],["Expropriation","A government taking a foreign company's property."],["Intellectual property","Legal rights over brands, inventions and creative work."]],
 mistakes:["Listing differences without saying what each one forces the company to change.","Using differences that are not in the case.","Saying 'culture is different' without naming the exact difference (religion, taste, language).","Mixing up a free trade area and a customs union."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"An Indian vegetarian snack brand plans to sell in Indonesia and Japan. What would it have to change in its product and promotion for each country, and why?",
  plan:["Difference 1: religion and food rules (Indonesia Muslim-majority, halal).","Change it forces: halal certification and labelling in Indonesia.","Difference 2: income and taste/packaging expectations (Japan high-income, high quality and small elegant packs).","Change it forces: premium small packs, mild flavours in Japan.","Short conclusion: keep the brand, adapt the details."],
  model:[["Difference 1","Indonesia is mainly Muslim, so food must be halal-certified. Even a vegetarian snack needs a recognised halal logo, because shoppers check it. The brand must get certification and show the logo clearly."],["Difference 2","Japan is a high-income market where buyers expect very high quality and neat, small packs, and many prefer milder flavours. The brand should offer smaller premium packs with less spice and careful Japanese labelling."],["Conclusion","The brand name and core snacks can stay the same, but certification, packaging, flavour and price must change for each market."]]},
 check:[["What is the difference between a free trade area and a customs union?","A customs union adds a common external tariff for non-members."],["Give one example of a high-context culture and one of a low-context culture.","High: Japan, Thailand, Arab countries. Low: Germany, the USA, Australia."],["What should a company do about its trademark before entering a new country?","Register it in that country."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student in the Maldives. Explain high-context and low-context cultures in simple English, with examples from Asia and the Gulf, and how each changes the way a company should sell.","Give me three pairs of countries. For each pair, ask me to name two differences that would make a food brand change its marketing. Correct my answers.","Explain free trade areas, customs unions, common markets and economic unions in simple English, with one real example of each."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 3 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[3]={wk:3,title:"Global Information Systems and Market Research",
 big:"Good decisions in a foreign market need good information: from systems, from research, and from seeing the market with your own eyes.",
 sum:"Management information systems and big data, sources of market information including direct perception, the market research process, and how global firms organise research.",
 min:[
  "A management information system (MIS) collects, stores and shares data so managers can make better decisions in every country.",
  "Big data means very large data sets (from sales, apps, social media) that firms analyse to find patterns.",
  "Information comes from inside the company, from outside sources, from secondary data, and from direct perception: going to the market to see it yourself.",
  "Secondary data is quick and cheap but may be old, missing or hard to compare between countries. Direct perception and primary research fill the gaps.",
  "The research process has clear steps, from defining the problem to presenting the findings."
 ],
 lo:"Explain how an MIS and big data support global decisions, describe sources of market information including direct perception, and outline the market research process and its adaptations abroad.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Management information systems and big data",p:"An MIS gives managers the right information, at the right time, across all countries. It links sales, stock, customer and market data.",
   l:[["Management information system (MIS)","A system that collects, analyses and shares data for decisions."],["Enterprise resource planning (ERP)","Software that links finance, production, stock and sales in one system."],["Customer relationship management (CRM)","Software that tracks every contact with each customer."],["Point-of-sale (POS) data","Sales data captured at the checkout, product by product."],["Big data","Huge, fast-changing data sets from many sources, analysed for patterns."],["Intranet / data warehouse","Internal networks and databases that let staff in all countries use the same data."]],
   eg:["Example: Amazon uses each customer's browsing and buying data in many countries to recommend products and plan stock.","Example: A Maldivian supermarket chain uses POS data from all its stores to see which imported brands sell fastest and reorder before stock runs out.","Example: Grab uses trip and order data across Southeast Asian cities to set prices and place drivers where demand is rising."],
   tip:"When asked how an MIS helps, give the decision it improves: what to make, where to send stock, what price to charge, which market to enter."},
  {h:"Sources of market information",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Where information comes from",cols:[["Internal",["Company staff abroad","Sales and customer records","Distributors' reports"]],["External / secondary",["Government statistics","Trade associations","World Bank, UN, research firms","Online reports and news"]],["Direct perception",["Managers visit the market","See shops, prices, customers","Talk to distributors and buyers"]]]},
   l:[["Secondary data","Data already collected by someone else for another purpose."],["Primary data","New data collected for this exact problem, for example surveys or interviews."],["Direct perception","Gaining information by going to the market and experiencing it first-hand: visiting, watching, listening and talking."]],
   eg:["Example: Before entering Bangladesh, a Thai snack company's managers walk through markets in Dhaka, see what children buy, check shelf prices and talk to shopkeepers. That is direct perception.","Example: A Maldivian fisheries exporter reads Japan's import statistics (secondary data), then sends its sales manager to a Tokyo seafood fair to meet buyers (direct perception)."]},
  {h:"Why combine secondary data with direct perception?",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Strengths and weaknesses",cols:[["Secondary data",["Quick and cheap","Shows size and trends","May be old or incomplete","Hard to compare across countries","Misses how people really behave"]],["Direct perception",["Real feel for culture and buying behaviour","Builds contacts","Takes time and travel money","One visit may not represent the whole market"]]]},
   tip:"The best answer says: secondary data first for the big picture, then direct perception to check it and add real understanding. Using both lowers the risk of a wrong decision."},
  {h:"The market research process",dia:{type:"flow",title:"Steps in market research",steps:[["Information requirements","What decision must we make, and what do we need to know?"],["Problem definition","Turn it into a clear research question."],["Choosing the unit of analysis","Which countries, regions or segments?"],["Examining data availability","What secondary data already exists?"],["Assessing the value of research","Will the answer be worth the cost?"],["Research design","Surveys, interviews, focus groups, observation."],["Data analysis","Look for patterns and differences between markets."],["Interpretation and presentation","Clear results and recommendations for managers."]]},
   l:[["Adapting research abroad","Translate and back-translate questionnaires; check that questions mean the same thing in each culture; choose methods that fit (phone, online, face-to-face)."],["Comparability","Make sure results from different countries can be compared fairly."]],
   eg:["Example: A survey about 'snacking' must be checked in each country: a 'snack' in India may be a small meal, while in Japan it may be a sweet."]},
  {h:"Organising research: multinational versus global firms",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Who runs the research?",cols:[["Multinational approach",["Each country does its own research","Little sharing; methods differ"]],["Global / transnational approach",["Research planned centrally","Done locally with shared methods","Results compared and shared across countries"]]]},
   eg:["Example: A global drinks company runs the same customer survey in 20 countries with one method, so it can compare results and spot ideas that travel."]},
  {h:"Information as a strategic asset",p:"Information has changed how global firms are organised. With shared data, decisions can be faster, layers of management can shrink, and teams in different countries can work together.",
   eg:["Example: A regional team in Singapore can watch live sales in eight countries and move stock or budget within days, instead of waiting for monthly reports."]}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Name the decision","What does the company need to decide (enter, price, target, product)?"],["Say what data it needs","Size, competitors, prices, tastes, rules."],["Pick sources","Secondary data for the big picture; direct perception or primary research for real behaviour."],["Note the risks","Old, missing or non-comparable data; cultural bias in surveys."],["Say how an MIS helps after entry","Track sales, stock and customers to adjust quickly."]]},
 terms:[["Management information system (MIS)","A system that collects, analyses and shares information for decisions."],["Big data","Very large, fast-changing data sets analysed for patterns."],["Secondary data","Existing data collected for another purpose."],["Primary data","New data collected for the current problem."],["Direct perception","Learning about a market by going there and experiencing it first-hand."],["Comparability","Whether research results from different countries can be fairly compared."],["Back-translation","Translating a questionnaire, then translating it back to check the meaning."]],
 mistakes:["Defining direct perception as 'reading reports'. It means going to the market in person.","Listing research steps without linking them to the decision.","Forgetting the weaknesses of secondary data in foreign markets.","Saying an MIS 'helps decisions' without naming a decision."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Maldivian handicraft brand wants to sell lacquerware and woven mats to gift shops in Dubai. Plan the information it should gather before deciding to enter, and say what each source would tell it.",
  plan:["Define secondary data and direct perception.","What secondary data tells it (tourist numbers, gift market, import rules).","Weaknesses of that data.","What direct perception adds (visit gift shops, prices, buyers).","Why using both reduces risk."],
  model:[["Define","Secondary data is information already collected by others, such as reports and statistics. Direct perception means managers go to the market and see it for themselves."],["Secondary data","The brand can read Dubai tourism statistics, reports on the gift and souvenir market, and UAE import rules. This is quick and cheap and shows the size of the market."],["Weakness","These reports may not cover small handicraft items and may be out of date."],["Direct perception","The owner could visit gift shops in Dubai malls and markets, check prices and displays, see which items tourists pick up, and meet shop buyers."],["Why both","Together they show whether the market is big enough and whether buyers really want Maldivian crafts at a workable price, so the decision is less risky."]]},
 check:[["What is direct perception?","Learning about a market by going there in person to see, hear and experience it."],["Give two weaknesses of secondary data in foreign markets.","Any two: may be old, incomplete, not comparable between countries, collected for a different purpose."],["What does back-translation check?","That a translated questionnaire keeps the same meaning."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain direct perception and secondary data in simple English, with a Maldivian business entering a foreign market as the example.","Explain how a management information system helps a global company make better decisions in different countries. Give me three examples, then quiz me.","Give me a short case of a company entering a new country and ask me to design the research steps. Correct my answer."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 4 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[4]={wk:4,title:"Segmentation, Targeting and Positioning",
 big:"Choose the right customers in each country, then give them one clear reason to choose you.",
 sum:"Ways to segment global markets, criteria for choosing target markets, the product-market grid, the three targeting strategies, and positioning options with positioning statements.",
 min:[
  "Segmentation splits a market into groups of customers with similar needs. Global marketers can segment by country or by customer type across countries.",
  "Common variables: demographic, psychographic, behavioural and benefit segmentation.",
  "To choose a target, check segment size and growth, competition, and compatibility and feasibility for the company.",
  "Three targeting strategies: standardised (one offer for all), concentrated (one segment) and differentiated (several segments, different offers).",
  "Positioning is the place the brand takes in customers' minds compared with competitors. A positioning statement says who it is for, what it is, how it is different, and why to believe it."
 ],
 lo:"Segment a foreign market, justify the choice of a target segment with evidence, choose a targeting strategy, and write a positioning statement.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Segmenting global markets",l:[["Demographic","Age, income, gender, family size, education. Example: global teens; the rising middle class in Asia."],["Psychographic","Values, attitudes, lifestyle. Example: health-focused young professionals."],["Behavioural","How people buy and use: user status, usage rate, loyalty, buying on impulse."],["Benefit","The main benefit people want: price, convenience, status, health, taste."]],
   eg:["Example: Global teens in Seoul, Jakarta and Colombo share music, games and fashion, so one youth campaign can reach them all.","Example: A sunscreen brand segments by benefit: sports users want water resistance; office workers want a light, invisible cream.","Example: A Maldivian dive company segments by behaviour: first-time divers want short guided dives; experienced divers want remote reefs and longer trips."],
   tip:"Global segments cross borders. A global segment is a group with similar needs in many countries."},
  {h:"Choosing target markets",dia:{type:"flow",title:"Three tests for a target",steps:[["Size and growth","Is it big enough now, or growing fast?"],["Competition","How strong are rivals? Is there a gap we can fill?"],["Compatibility and feasibility","Does it fit our strengths, budget and brand? Can we reach it?"]]},
   l:[["Product-market grid","A table of countries (or segments) against the company's products, used to compare options and pick targets."]],
   eg:["Example: A Malaysian batik fashion label compares Singapore, Indonesia and the UAE on market size, competitors and how easy shipping is, then picks the best two."]},
  {h:"Three targeting strategies",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Targeting strategies",cols:[["Standardised (mass)",["One offer for a broad market","Low cost","Risk: fits no one perfectly"]],["Concentrated",["Focus on one segment","Strong fit and expertise","Risk: all eggs in one basket"]],["Differentiated",["Two or more segments, different offers","Covers more of the market","Risk: higher cost and complexity"]]]},
   eg:["Example: A luxury watch maker uses concentrated targeting: wealthy buyers in many countries.","Example: A car maker uses differentiated targeting: small cheap cars for first-time buyers, large SUVs for families."]},
  {h:"Justifying a target with evidence",p:"Questions often ask why one group is a better first target. Use the three tests and the case facts.",
   l:["Need fit: what the group wants matches what the product offers.","Buying behaviour: how often, how much, impulse or planned.","Ability to pay: price fits their budget.","Reach: the company can reach them with its channels.","Competition: fewer strong rivals in this group.","Compare: say why another group is weaker."],
   eg:["Example: For a new Korean instant noodle brand in Pakistan, university students are a better first target than parents of young children: students try new flavours, buy single packs often, and share on social media; parents prefer familiar brands for children."]},
  {h:"Positioning",l:[["Attribute or benefit","Known for one feature or benefit: 'the longest-lasting battery'."],["Quality and price","Premium quality at a high price, or good value."],["Use or user","Linked to a use ('for after the gym') or a type of user ('for students')."],["Competitor","Compared directly with a rival: 'the healthier choice than fried snacks'."],["Global consumer culture positioning","Shown as a global brand used by modern people everywhere."],["Local consumer culture positioning","Linked to local culture, values and life."],["Foreign consumer culture positioning","Linked to a foreign country's image: 'Japanese precision', 'Italian style'."]],
   eg:["Example: Sony uses global consumer culture positioning: the same modern image everywhere.","Example: A Maldivian coconut oil brand selling in Europe uses foreign consumer culture positioning: 'pure island coconut from the Maldives'."]},
  {h:"Writing a positioning statement",p:"A simple, reliable format:",
   dia:{type:"flow",title:"Positioning statement template",steps:[["For","the target customer and their need"],["[Brand] is the","product category or frame of reference"],["that","main benefit or point of difference"],["Unlike","the main competitors"],["because","the reason to believe (proof)"]]},
   eg:["Example: 'For busy young office workers in Colombo who want a healthy lunch, FreshBox is the ready-meal subscription that delivers a balanced Sri Lankan meal to your desk by noon. Unlike fast food, every meal is planned by a nutritionist and cooked fresh that morning.'"],
   tip:"A strong statement names the target, the category, one clear difference and a reason to believe. It should fit the gap in the market described in the case."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["List the segments","Use the groups the case describes."],["Test each","Size and growth, competition, fit and reach."],["Pick and compare","Choose one; say why another is weaker, with case facts."],["Choose the strategy","Concentrated, differentiated or standardised."],["Write the positioning","For / is the / that / unlike / because."]]},
 terms:[["Segmentation","Dividing a market into groups of customers with similar needs."],["Global market segment","A group of customers with similar needs in many countries."],["Psychographic segmentation","Grouping by values, attitudes and lifestyle."],["Behavioural segmentation","Grouping by how people buy and use a product."],["Benefit segmentation","Grouping by the main benefit customers want."],["Targeting","Choosing which segments to serve."],["Product-market grid","A table comparing markets or segments with products to choose targets."],["Concentrated targeting","Focusing on one segment."],["Differentiated targeting","Serving several segments with different offers."],["Positioning","The place a brand holds in customers' minds compared with rivals."],["Positioning statement","A short statement of target, category, difference and proof."]],
 mistakes:["Choosing a target without case evidence.","Not saying why other groups are weaker.","A positioning statement with no competitor or no point of difference.","Mixing up segmentation (splitting) and targeting (choosing)."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Sri Lankan spice brand wants to sell ready-to-cook curry kits in the United Kingdom. Which customers should it aim at first in the UK, and how should it present the kits so they stand out from what is already on the shelf?",
  plan:["Pick a segment: young professionals who like Asian food but cook quickly.","Justify with needs, ability to pay and reach (supermarkets, online).","Say how to reach them (where they shop, what media they use).","Write the statement: for / is the / that / unlike / because."],
  model:[["Target","The best first target is young UK professionals aged 25–40 who enjoy Asian food but have little time to cook."],["Justify","They want quick, tasty meals, can pay a premium for convenience, and shop in supermarkets and online where the kits can be sold."],["Reach","They shop in large supermarkets and online grocery apps, and follow food creators on social media, so the kits can be sold and promoted where they already are."],["Positioning","'For busy young professionals in the UK who love Asian food, SpiceTrail is the curry kit that makes a real Sri Lankan curry in 20 minutes. Unlike jar sauces, it uses freshly ground spices from Sri Lankan farms, packed in the right amounts for each recipe.'"]]},
 check:[["Name the three tests for choosing a target market.","Size and growth, competition, compatibility and feasibility."],["What is concentrated targeting?","Focusing on one segment."],["What are the parts of a positioning statement?","Target, category, point of difference, competitor, reason to believe."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain segmentation, targeting and positioning in simple English, using an Asian brand entering a new country as the example.","Give me a short case with three customer groups. Ask me to choose the best first target and justify it. Then mark my answer.","Help me practise positioning statements. Give me three products and markets, and correct the statements I write."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 5 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[5]={wk:5,title:"Importing, Exporting and Sourcing",
 big:"Selling abroad and making things abroad are both choices: where to sell, where to make, and how governments help or block you.",
 sum:"Export selling versus export marketing, government export support and import barriers, export organisations, payment methods, and sourcing decisions.",
 min:[
  "Export selling just sells the home product abroad. Export marketing studies the foreign customer and adapts the marketing mix.",
  "Governments support exports with tax breaks, finance, information and trade missions, and restrict imports with tariffs, quotas and other barriers.",
  "Exporters use intermediaries such as agents, distributors, export merchants and freight forwarders.",
  "Payment methods range from safest for the seller (cash with order, also called cash in advance) to riskiest (open account). Letters of credit are common for new partners.",
  "Sourcing means deciding where to make products. The textbook names eight factors: management vision, factor costs and conditions, customer needs, public opinion, logistics, country infrastructure, the political environment and exchange rates."
 ],
 lo:"Compare export selling and export marketing, explain how governments support exports and restrict imports, describe export organisations and payment methods, and analyse sourcing decisions.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Export selling versus export marketing",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Two ways to export",cols:[["Export selling",["Same product, price and promotion as at home","Focus on making the sale","Little research"]],["Export marketing",["Starts with the foreign customer's needs","Adapts product, price, channel and promotion","Research and planning first"]]]},
   eg:["Example: A Maldivian coconut-oil maker that ships the same jars to anyone who orders is export selling. If it studies German buyers, adds organic certification and a German label, it is export marketing."],
   tip:"Export marketing is the better long-term approach; say why: it fits the customer, so it builds repeat sales."},
  {h:"Government export support",l:[["Tax incentives","Lower taxes on export earnings."],["Subsidies and export finance","Cheap loans, credit insurance or grants for exporters."],["Information and training","Market reports, export training and advice."],["Trade missions and fairs","Help to meet foreign buyers at events abroad."],["Free trade zones / special economic zones","Areas with lower taxes and simpler customs for export production."]],
   eg:["Example: Japan's JETRO gives market information and helps small Japanese firms meet overseas buyers.","Example: Sri Lanka's Export Development Board runs promotion events for tea, spices and apparel abroad."]},
  {h:"Government import restrictions",l:[["Tariffs","Taxes on imported goods; they raise the price of imports."],["Quotas","Limits on the amount of a product that can be imported."],["Non-tariff barriers","Rules that make importing harder: complex standards, testing, licences, labelling rules, slow customs."],["Local content rules","A share of the product must be made locally."],["Embargoes and sanctions","Complete bans on trade with a country or in a product."]],
   eg:["Example: Many countries charge high tariffs on imported cars to protect local car makers.","Example: Strict food-safety testing and labelling rules can act as a non-tariff barrier for small food exporters."],
   tip:"For 'two ways to support exports and two ways to restrict imports', keep them in two clear lists, and give one example."},
  {h:"Organisations in the export process",l:[["Export agent","Finds buyers for the exporter, earns commission, does not own the goods."],["Distributor","Buys the goods, holds stock and resells in the foreign market."],["Export merchant","Buys goods at home and sells them abroad on its own account."],["Freight forwarder","Arranges shipping, documents and customs for the exporter."],["Home-country export department","The exporter's own team at home that handles foreign sales."],["Market-country organisation","A sales office, branch or subsidiary in the foreign market."]],
   eg:["Example: A small Indian textile firm first uses an export agent in Europe, then opens a sales office in Germany once sales grow."]},
  {h:"Export payment methods",dia:{type:"flow",title:"From safest to riskiest for the exporter",steps:[["Cash with order (CWO)","Buyer pays before shipping (cash in advance). Safest for the seller."],["Letter of credit (L/C)","The buyer's bank promises payment once the documents are correct."],["Documentary collection","Banks exchange documents for payment (D/P) or for a promise to pay later (D/A)."],["Open account","Goods are shipped and the buyer pays later. Riskiest for the seller."],["Consignment","The seller is paid only after the goods are sold."]]},
   eg:["Example: A Thai rice exporter asks a new buyer in Africa for a letter of credit, then moves to open account after a few years of trust."]},
  {h:"Sourcing decisions: where to make it",l:[["Management vision","Some leaders want to make products at home; others make them wherever is best."],["Factor costs and conditions","Land, labour, materials and capital costs, and the skills and technology available."],["Customer needs","Some customers need fast delivery, local service or special versions, which pulls production closer to them."],["Public opinion","People at home may object when jobs move abroad, or when a supplier has poor working conditions."],["Logistics","Distance, transport time and cost, freshness, stock levels."],["Country infrastructure","Ports, roads, power, internet."],["Political environment","Stability, rule changes, and trade barriers such as tariffs. Making inside a trade bloc avoids tariffs between members."],["Exchange rates","Currency moves change the cost of making in a country."]],
   eg:["Example: Dyson moved much of its manufacturing to Malaysia and Singapore to be close to its Asian suppliers and growing markets.","Example: Toyota makes pick-up trucks in Thailand and exports them across Asia, using Thailand's car-parts suppliers and ASEAN trade links.","Example: Many electronics companies have added factories in India to reach its huge market and spread their risk."],
   tip:"Pick factors that the case gives facts for, and use the numbers (cost per unit, days to deliver)."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Name the decision","Export from home, use a partner, or make abroad?"],["Compare locations","Cost, time, infrastructure, quality, risk, market access."],["Use the numbers","Cost per unit, transport days, tariffs."],["Check government rules","Support available; barriers to face."],["Recommend","Choose a location or method and say why it beats the alternative."]]},
 terms:[["Export selling","Selling the home product abroad without changing the marketing mix."],["Export marketing","Adapting the marketing mix to the foreign customer."],["Tariff","A tax on imported goods."],["Quota","A limit on the amount that can be imported."],["Non-tariff barrier","A rule other than a tax that makes importing harder."],["Freight forwarder","A firm that arranges shipping, documents and customs."],["Letter of credit","A bank's promise to pay the exporter once documents are correct."],["Open account","Shipping goods and letting the buyer pay later."],["Sourcing","Deciding where and by whom products are made."],["Free trade zone","An area with lower taxes and simpler customs for trade and production."]],
 mistakes:["Listing sourcing factors with no example or case link.","Mixing up an agent (no ownership) and a distributor (buys and resells).","Calling a tariff a quota.","Forgetting exchange rates and logistics when comparing locations."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Maldivian bottled-water company plans to sell in Sri Lanka and India. Should it bottle in the Maldives and export, or use a bottling partner in India? Weigh up the options and recommend one.",
  plan:["Factor 1: cost (shipping heavy water is expensive).","Factor 2: logistics and time (closer production, faster delivery).","Factor 3: market access and rules (tariffs, local standards).","Weigh the factors.","Recommend with a reason."],
  model:[["Cost","Water is heavy and cheap, so shipping it from the Maldives adds a lot to the cost of every bottle. A partner in India avoids most of this cost."],["Logistics","Bottling near the customers means faster delivery and less stock waiting in ports."],["Market access and rules","India charges import duties on many goods and has its own food-safety standards. Local bottling avoids import duty and makes certification easier."],["Recommend","The company should use a bottling partner in India, with its own quality standards and brand, and keep Maldivian bottling for the home market. This keeps prices competitive while protecting quality."]]},
 check:[["Which payment method is safest for the exporter?","Cash with order (payment in advance)."],["Give two non-tariff barriers.","Any two: strict standards or testing, licences, labelling rules, slow customs, local content rules."],["What is the difference between export selling and export marketing?","Export marketing adapts the mix to foreign customers; export selling does not."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain export payment methods from safest to riskiest in simple English, with a short example for each.","Give me three short cases about companies choosing where to make their products. Ask me to name three sourcing factors for each. Correct my answers.","Explain how governments support exports and restrict imports, with Asian examples. Then quiz me."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 6 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[6]={wk:6,title:"Global Market-Entry Strategies: Licensing, Investment and Strategic Alliances",
 big:"How a company enters a country is a trade-off between control, cost, risk and speed.",
 sum:"Licensing and franchising, contract manufacturing, joint ventures, equity stakes, wholly owned subsidiaries, strategic alliances, cooperative strategies in Asia, and market expansion strategies.",
 min:[
  "Entry modes run from low control and low risk (exporting, licensing) to high control and high risk (wholly owned subsidiaries).",
  "Licensing and franchising let a partner use your brand or know-how for a fee: fast and cheap, but less control.",
  "Contract manufacturing lets another firm make your product while you keep the brand and marketing.",
  "Joint ventures share cost, risk and local knowledge with a partner, but control is shared too.",
  "Wholly owned subsidiaries (new builds or acquisitions) give full control at the highest cost and risk.",
  "Every mode that shares know-how needs protection: contracts, registered trademarks and quality checks."
 ],
 lo:"Explain the advantages and disadvantages of each entry mode, recommend a mode for a company with evidence, and explain how to protect brand and know-how.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"The entry-mode ladder",dia:{type:"flow",title:"More control, more cost and risk",steps:[["Exporting","Sell from home through agents or distributors."],["Licensing / franchising","A partner uses your brand or know-how for a fee."],["Contract manufacturing","A partner makes the product; you keep brand and marketing."],["Joint venture / equity stake","You and a partner share ownership."],["Wholly owned subsidiary","You own 100%: build new (greenfield) or buy (acquisition)."]]},
   tip:"Recommend a mode by matching it to the company's money, experience, need for control and the market's risk and rules."},
  {h:"Licensing and franchising",l:[["Licensing","A licensor lets a licensee use patents, trademarks, technology or know-how in return for a royalty."],["Franchising","A franchisor gives a franchisee a full business system: brand, products, operations, training and support, for fees and royalties."],["Master franchise","One partner gets the rights for a whole country or region, and runs or sub-franchises the outlets."]],
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Licensing and franchising",cols:[["Advantages",["Fast entry, low investment","Partner knows the local market","Low risk for the brand owner"]],["Disadvantages",["Less control over quality","Lower share of profit","Partner may become a competitor","Know-how can leak"]]]},
   eg:["Example: Tokyo Disneyland is owned and run by a Japanese company under a licence from Disney, which earns royalties.","Example: Domino's Pizza entered India through a master franchise with Jubilant FoodWorks, which runs the stores across the country."]},
  {h:"Contract manufacturing",p:"Contract manufacturing means another company makes the product to your design and quality standards. You keep the brand, the marketing and the customer.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Contract manufacturing",cols:[["Advantages",["No factory investment","Lower cost per unit and faster delivery near the market","Use a partner's skills and certifications","Easy to scale up or down"]],["Disadvantages",["Less control over production","Risk of quality problems","Risk the partner copies the product","Depends on the partner's reliability"]]]},
   eg:["Example: Nike owns almost no factories. Contract manufacturers in Asia make its shoes to Nike's designs and standards.","Example: Many pharmaceutical brands use contract manufacturers in India to make medicines to their formulas."]},
  {h:"Investment: joint ventures, equity stakes and full ownership",l:[["Joint venture (JV)","Two or more firms create a new company and share ownership, cost, risk and profit."],["Equity stake","Buying part of a foreign company to gain influence or access."],["Wholly owned subsidiary","A company owns 100% of its foreign business."],["Greenfield investment","Building a new operation from scratch."],["Acquisition","Buying an existing company in the market."]],
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Joint venture or full ownership?",cols:[["Joint venture",["Shared cost and risk","Partner's local knowledge and contacts","Helps with foreign ownership rules","Risk: disputes, shared control and profit"]],["Wholly owned",["Full control of brand and quality","All the profit","Highest cost and risk","Slower to learn the market alone"]]]},
   eg:["Example: Maruti Suzuki began as a joint venture between Suzuki of Japan and the Indian government, giving Suzuki local knowledge and support.","Example: When Walmart bought a majority of Flipkart in India, it entered by acquisition."]},
  {h:"Global strategic partnerships",p:"A strategic alliance is a partnership between firms, often competitors, to reach goals neither could reach alone, while each stays independent.",
   l:[["Success factors","Clear shared mission and strategy, good governance, cultural fit, and trust between partners."],["Challenges in developing countries","Unstable rules, weak legal protection, different business cultures, partners who want control, and protecting technology."]],
   eg:["Example: Star Alliance links many airlines so passengers can fly one ticket across networks.","Example: Renault and Nissan share car platforms and buying power while staying separate companies."]},
  {h:"Cooperative strategies in Asia",l:[["Keiretsu (Japan)","Groups of companies linked by shared ownership, banks and long-term supply ties, for example the Toyota supplier group."],["Chaebol (South Korea)","Large family-controlled business groups across many industries, for example Samsung and Hyundai."]],
   eg:["Example: A foreign supplier wanting to sell to a Japanese car maker may need years to win a place in its keiretsu supply network."]},
  {h:"Market expansion strategies",p:"Companies also decide how many countries and how many market segments to serve.",
   dia:{type:"grid",title:"Market expansion strategies",yl:"Country",y:["Concentration","Diversification"],xl:"Market segment",x:["Concentration","Diversification"],cells:[["Few countries, few segments","Narrow focus, common for new exporters."],["Few countries, many segments","Deep in a few countries."],["Many countries, few segments","A global niche: one segment worldwide."],["Many countries, many segments","Large global companies."]]},
   eg:["Example: A luxury dive-resort brand serves one segment (serious divers) in many countries: country diversification, market concentration."]},
  {h:"Protecting your brand and know-how",l:["Register trademarks and patents in every country before entry.","Sign contracts with confidentiality and non-compete clauses.","Share only part of the know-how, or keep a key ingredient or component made at home.","Approve every batch; audit partners and train staff.","Choose trusted partners with a good record."],
   tip:"Give one safeguard and explain how it reduces the specific risk in the case."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Read the constraints","Money, experience, control needed, time, market rules."],["Compare two or three modes","Use the advantages and disadvantages tables."],["Use case facts","Costs, partner strengths, risks."],["Recommend one mode","Say why it beats the next-best option."],["Add a safeguard","How the brand and know-how stay protected."]]},
 terms:[["Licensing","Letting a partner use your patents, trademarks or know-how for a royalty."],["Franchising","Giving a partner a full business system for fees and royalties."],["Master franchise","Rights for a whole country or region given to one partner."],["Contract manufacturing","Another firm makes your product to your standards; you keep the brand."],["Joint venture","A new company owned jointly by two or more firms."],["Equity stake","Owning part of a foreign company."],["Wholly owned subsidiary","A foreign business owned 100% by the company."],["Greenfield investment","Building a new operation from scratch."],["Strategic alliance","A partnership between independent firms for shared goals."],["Keiretsu","A Japanese group of companies linked by ownership and supply ties."],["Chaebol","A large family-controlled Korean business group."]],
 mistakes:["Defining a mode without applying it to the company.","Recommending full ownership for a small firm with little money.","Forgetting the risks of sharing know-how.","Mixing up licensing (rights to use) and franchising (a whole business system)."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Maldivian surf-wear brand wants to enter Indonesia but has little capital and no staff there. Recommend an entry mode and explain one risk and one safeguard.",
  plan:["Constraints: little capital, no local staff, new market.","Compare: exporting via distributor, licensing, joint venture.","Recommend: licensing (or a distributor) to a local surf retailer.","Risk: quality and brand control.","Safeguard: contract terms, approvals, registered trademark."],
  model:[["Recommend","The brand should license its designs and name to an established Indonesian surf-wear retailer, which would make and sell the products locally."],["Why","Licensing needs little capital and no local staff, and the partner already knows surf shoppers in Bali and Lombok. It is faster and cheaper than a joint venture or its own stores."],["Risk","The partner may use cheaper fabrics or print designs badly, which would damage the brand."],["Safeguard","Register the trademark in Indonesia first. The licence contract should set fabric and print standards, require approval of samples before each season, and allow the brand to end the licence if standards slip."]]},
 check:[["What is the main advantage of a wholly owned subsidiary?","Full control of brand, quality and profit."],["What is a master franchise?","One partner gets franchise rights for a whole country or region."],["Name one way to protect know-how when using a partner.","Any one: register trademarks, confidentiality and non-compete clauses, share only part of the know-how, batch approval and audits."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain licensing, franchising, contract manufacturing, joint ventures and wholly owned subsidiaries in simple English with one Asian example each.","Give me three short company cases and ask me to recommend an entry mode for each, with one risk and one safeguard. Mark my answers.","Explain keiretsu and chaebol in simple English and how they affect foreign companies."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 7 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[7]={wk:7,title:"Brand and Product Decisions in Global Marketing",
 big:"Keep the brand's core the same everywhere, and change only what customers in each country need changed.",
 sum:"Basic product and brand concepts, local, international and global brands, Maslow's needs, country of origin, the five product and communication strategies, brand dilution, and the new-product continuum.",
 min:[
  "A product is everything the customer gets: the physical item, the brand, packaging, warranty and service.",
  "Brands can be local, international or global. Global brands use the same name and a similar image everywhere.",
  "Maslow's hierarchy helps explain why buyers in different countries want different benefits from the same product.",
  "Country of origin can add or remove value from a brand.",
  "Five strategic alternatives combine extending or adapting the product with extending or adapting the communication.",
  "Selling one brand in very different channels or price levels can dilute (weaken) its image.",
  "New products range from small improvements (continuous) to things that change how people live (discontinuous)."
 ],
 lo:"Explain product and brand concepts, choose between the five product/communication strategies, assess country of origin and brand dilution risks, and classify innovations.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Product and brand basics",l:[["Product","A good, service or idea with physical and psychological value: features, design, brand, packaging, service."],["Brand","A name, sign or design, plus the promises and feelings customers link to it."],["Brand equity","The extra value a brand adds: why people pay more for it."],["Brand image","How customers see the brand."],["Packaging and labelling","Protects, informs and promotes; must meet local language and law."]],
   eg:["Example: Customers pay more for a Toyota than for a little-known car with similar features, because they trust Toyota's reliability: that is brand equity."]},
  {h:"Local, international and global brands",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Three kinds of brand",cols:[["Local",["Sold in one country","Built for local taste"]],["International",["Sold in several countries or a region","Some adaptation"]],["Global",["Same name and similar image worldwide","Strong recognition","Scale savings"]]]},
   eg:["Example (local): A Maldivian bakery brand sold only in Malé.","Example (international): A Thai drinks brand sold across ASEAN.","Example (global): Coca-Cola, Samsung and Uniqlo."]},
  {h:"Maslow's hierarchy and global products",p:"People in different countries may be at different levels of need, so they want different benefits.",
   dia:{type:"pyramid",title:"Maslow's hierarchy of needs",levels:[["Self-actualisation","growth, fulfilment"],["Esteem","status, respect"],["Social","belonging, love"],["Safety","security, protection"],["Physiological","food, water, shelter"]]},
   eg:["Example: In a lower-income market, a bar of soap is sold for hygiene and health (safety). In a high-income market, a luxury soap is sold as a treat or status item (esteem)."]},
  {h:"Country of origin",p:"Country of origin is the country customers link with a product. It can be a strength or a weakness.",
   l:["Positive: German cars (engineering), Japanese electronics (reliability), Italian fashion (style), French perfume (luxury), Korean beauty (innovation).","Negative or unknown: a country with no reputation for that product may need to build trust with certifications, quality signals or a partner's name."],
   eg:["Example: A Maldivian sea-salt brand can use 'from the clean Indian Ocean' as a positive country-of-origin story.","Example: Few people link the Maldives with manufactured goods, so a Maldivian electronics brand would gain little from 'Made in the Maldives'."],
   tip:"Say whether the country image helps or hurts THIS product, and how the brand should use or manage it."},
  {h:"Five product and communication strategies",dia:{type:"flow",title:"Five strategic alternatives",steps:[["1. Product–communication extension (dual extension)","Same product, same message everywhere."],["2. Product extension–communication adaptation","Same product, different message (the product meets a different need)."],["3. Product adaptation–communication extension","Product changes, same message."],["4. Dual adaptation","Both product and message change."],["5. Innovation (product invention)","Create a new product for the market's needs, often at a price people with lower incomes can afford."]]},
   eg:["Example (1): Red Bull sells the same can with the same message everywhere.","Example (3): KitKat keeps its 'Have a break' message but Japan has many local flavours such as matcha.","Example (4): A noodle brand changes both the flavour and the ad story for each Asian country.","Example (5): A low-cost water filter designed for homes without clean tap water is a product invention for that market.","Example (2): A bicycle sold for fun in Europe may be sold as everyday transport in South Asia: same product, different message."],
   tip:"First decide: does the product change? Does the message change? Then name the strategy."},
  {h:"Global brand, local changes, and brand dilution",p:["Many brands keep the core (name, logo, signature products) and add local items.","Brand dilution happens when a brand loses its special image, for example when a premium brand appears too often, too cheaply, or in low-cost channels."],
   l:[["Protect the premium","Keep some products, packs or experiences only in premium channels."],["Separate lines","Use sub-brands or different pack sizes for mass channels."],["Control price position","Keep the price gap to rivals the same in every channel."],["Consistent quality","The cheapest item must still meet the brand's quality promise."]],
   eg:["Example: Luxury fashion houses limit discounts and outlet sales so the brand stays exclusive.","Example: Toyota sells its luxury cars under the separate Lexus brand, so the mass-market Toyota image does not pull the luxury cars down."]},
  {h:"The new-product continuum",dia:{type:"flow",title:"Types of innovation",steps:[["Continuous innovation","A small change to an existing product: a new flavour or size. Little change in behaviour."],["Dynamically continuous innovation","A bigger change that alters behaviour somewhat: wireless earbuds."],["Discontinuous innovation","A new product that changes how people live or work: the first mobile phones."]]},
   eg:["Example: A new mango flavour of a yoghurt is continuous innovation."]}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["What stays the same?","Brand name, core product, quality promise."],["What changes, and why?","Flavour, size, packaging, message: link each change to a country fact."],["Name the strategy","Which of the five alternatives?"],["Check the risks","Brand dilution, weak country-of-origin image."],["Protect the brand","Separate lines, premium-only items, consistent quality."]]},
 terms:[["Brand equity","The extra value a brand name adds to a product."],["Global brand","Same name and similar image in many countries."],["Country of origin","The country customers link with a product, which affects how they value it."],["Product–communication extension","Same product and same message everywhere."],["Product adaptation–communication extension","Change the product, keep the message."],["Product extension–communication adaptation","Keep the product, change the message."],["Dual adaptation","Change both the product and the message."],["Product invention","Develop a new product for a market's needs."],["Brand dilution","Weakening of a brand's image, often from overexposure or cheap channels."],["Continuous innovation","A small change to an existing product."],["Discontinuous innovation","A new product that changes behaviour."]],
 mistakes:["Mixing up strategies 2 and 3. Ask: did the product change or the message?","Saying country of origin is always positive.","Explaining brand dilution without saying how to prevent it.","Calling a new flavour a discontinuous innovation."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"An Indian designer label sells handwoven silk sarees in its own boutiques in Mumbai, Dubai and London. A large online discount app offers to sell its sarees with frequent flash sales. Explain how this could harm the brand and suggest two ways the label could still use online sales safely.",
  plan:["Explain brand dilution for this label.","Link it to discounting and mass exposure.","Way 1: sell online only through its own website or a premium marketplace.","Way 2: a separate, clearly named everyday line for the app; no flash sales on signature pieces.","Short conclusion."],
  model:[["Harm","Frequent flash sales next to cheap mass-market clothes would make the label look ordinary. Boutique customers who pay full price might feel the brand has lost its exclusivity. This is brand dilution."],["Way 1","The label could sell online through its own website and premium fashion platforms that do not discount, keeping the same prices as its boutiques."],["Way 2","If it still wants the discount app's reach, it could create a separate everyday cotton line with its own name, and never offer the signature silk sarees there."],["Conclusion","This lets the label reach new online buyers while the boutique experience and signature pieces stay special."]]},
 check:[["Same product, new message for a different use: which strategy?","Product extension–communication adaptation."],["Give one positive country-of-origin example.","Any one: German cars, Japanese electronics, Italian fashion, French perfume, Korean beauty."],["What is brand dilution?","Weakening of a brand's image, for example through cheap channels or overexposure."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain the five product and communication strategies in simple English with an Asian brand example for each. Then quiz me with four short cases.","Explain brand dilution and how a premium brand can protect itself when selling in mass channels in another country.","Explain country of origin effects with examples from Asia, and ask me whether 'from the Maldives' would help three different products."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 8 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[8]={wk:8,title:"Pricing Decisions",
 big:"A global price must cover costs, match the brand's position, suit local incomes and rules, and stop goods flowing from cheap countries to expensive ones.",
 sum:"Pricing basics and objectives, Incoterms, environmental influences, ethnocentric/polycentric/geocentric pricing, grey markets, dumping, price fixing, transfer pricing and countertrade.",
 min:[
  "Price must sit between cost (the floor) and what customers will pay (the ceiling), and fit the brand's position against competitors.",
  "Pricing objectives include market skimming, penetration pricing and pricing for companion products.",
  "Incoterms decide who pays for transport, insurance and duty, so they change the final price.",
  "Exchange rates, inflation, government rules and competition all push prices up or down in each country.",
  "Three approaches: ethnocentric (one world price), polycentric (each country sets its own) and geocentric (one position, local adjustment).",
  "Big price gaps between countries create grey markets. Selling abroad below home price or below cost is dumping.",
  "Transfer pricing sets prices between branches of the same company; countertrade pays with goods instead of money."
 ],
 lo:"Explain pricing objectives and influences, apply the ethnocentric/polycentric/geocentric framework, and explain grey markets, dumping, price fixing, transfer pricing and countertrade.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Pricing basics and objectives",l:[["Market skimming","Start with a high price to earn high margins from buyers who want the product most; lower it later."],["Penetration pricing","Start with a low price to win market share quickly."],["Companion products","Price one product low and earn on the items used with it (printers and ink, game consoles and games)."],["Target costing","Decide the price the market will pay first, then design the product to make a profit at that price."]],
   eg:["Example: A new premium phone launches at a high price in Singapore (skimming), while a budget brand enters India with very low prices (penetration)."]},
  {h:"Incoterms",p:["Incoterms are international rules that say who pays for transport, insurance and duties, and where the risk passes from seller to buyer.","They come in four groups. E-terms: the buyer collects at the seller's door. F-terms: the seller hands the goods to the buyer's carrier (FCA, FAS, FOB). C-terms: the seller pays the main transport (CFR, CIF and others). D-terms: the seller delivers to the destination (DDP and others). The four below are the ones you meet most often."],
   dia:{type:"flow",title:"From least to most seller responsibility",steps:[["EXW (Ex Works)","Buyer collects at the seller's premises and pays everything after."],["FOB (Free On Board)","Seller pays until goods are loaded on the ship."],["CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight)","Seller pays shipping and insurance to the destination port."],["DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)","Seller pays everything, including import duty, to the buyer's door."]]},
   eg:["Example: A Maldivian exporter quoting CIF Colombo includes freight and insurance in its price, so the price is higher than an FOB Malé quote."],
   tip:"Price escalation: every extra cost (freight, insurance, duty, distributor margins) adds up, so the shelf price abroad can be far higher than at home."},
  {h:"Environmental influences on price",l:[["Exchange rates","If the home currency strengthens, exports become more expensive abroad."],["Inflation","Prices must rise in high-inflation countries to protect margins."],["Government controls","Price caps, taxes, subsidies and import duties."],["Competition","Local rivals and substitutes limit how high price can go."],["Income levels","Lower incomes mean smaller packs or lower prices."]],
   eg:["Example: When a country's currency falls sharply, an importer must raise shelf prices or accept lower profit."]},
  {h:"Ethnocentric, polycentric and geocentric pricing",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Three pricing approaches",cols:[["Ethnocentric",["One price everywhere (home price converted)","Simple","Ignores local incomes and costs","Often too high or too low"]],["Polycentric",["Each country sets its own price","Fits local conditions","Big gaps between countries","Grey market risk; brand image varies"]],["Geocentric",["One price position against competitors in every country","Each country adjusts the actual price for incomes, taxes, margins and exchange rates","Consistent brand and fewer grey-market gaps"]]]},
   eg:["Example (ethnocentric): A European furniture maker converts its euro price list to every currency and changes nothing.","Example (polycentric): A drinks company lets each country's distributor set any price, so a can costs three times more in one country than next door.","Example (geocentric): A smartphone brand always prices its mid-range model just below the market leader's mid-range phone in each country. The actual price differs by country, but the position is the same."],
   tip:"Geocentric is usually the best answer for a regional or global brand. Say what stays the same (the position) and what changes (the actual price), and how it limits grey markets."},
  {h:"Grey markets",p:"Grey market goods are genuine branded products sold through channels the brand did not authorise, usually because they are bought cheaply in one country and resold in another.",
   l:["Narrow the price gaps between nearby countries (geocentric pricing).","Use different packaging, labels or models for each country.","Limit warranties to authorised sellers.","Track serial numbers and stop distributors who sell outside their area.","Act against unauthorised online sellers."],
   eg:["Example: Phones bought cheaply in one country and sold online in a more expensive one, without a local warranty, are grey-market goods."]},
  {h:"Dumping",p:"Dumping is selling a product abroad at a price below its price in the home market, or below its cost.",
   dia:{type:"compare",title:"Dumping or normal low price?",cols:[["Dumping",["Below the home-market price or below cost","Often aims to push out local rivals","Can be met with anti-dumping duties under WTO rules"]],["Normal low price",["Comes from lower costs or efficiency","Still covers costs","Legal competition"]]]},
   eg:["Example (hypothetical): A tile maker sells tiles abroad at half its home price to win share; the importing country investigates and adds an anti-dumping duty."],
   tip:"Governments act against dumping because it can close local firms and cost jobs."},
  {h:"Price fixing, transfer pricing and countertrade",l:[["Horizontal price fixing","Competitors agree on prices. Illegal in most countries."],["Vertical price fixing","A maker forces retailers to sell at a set price. Restricted in many countries."],["Transfer pricing","The price one part of a company charges another part in a different country for goods, services or brand use. It moves profit between countries, so tax authorities check it is 'arm's length' (what independent firms would charge)."],["Countertrade","Paying with goods instead of money. Forms: barter (direct swap), counterpurchase, offset (common in defence deals), compensation trading or buyback, and switch trading."]],
   eg:["Example (transfer pricing, hypothetical): A group's factory in a low-tax country charges its sales branch in a high-tax country a very high price for goods, so profit appears in the low-tax country. Tax officers can challenge this.","Example (countertrade): A country short of US dollars pays for imported machinery partly with tea or rubber."]}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Find the price facts","Home price, costs, local incomes, competitor price levels, taxes, exchange rates."],["Name the approach","Ethnocentric, polycentric or geocentric, with evidence."],["Place the brand","Where should it sit against competitors in each market?"],["Check the risks","Grey market, dumping rules, currency changes."],["Recommend","One position, local adjustment, regular price reviews."]]},
 terms:[["Market skimming","A high launch price to earn high margins first."],["Penetration pricing","A low launch price to win share quickly."],["Incoterms","Rules on who pays transport, insurance and duty, and when risk passes."],["Price escalation","The rise in price abroad as transport, duty and margins add up."],["Ethnocentric pricing","One price everywhere, usually the home price converted."],["Polycentric pricing","Each country sets its own price."],["Geocentric pricing","One price position worldwide, with local adjustment of the actual price."],["Grey market","Genuine goods sold through unauthorised channels, often across borders."],["Dumping","Selling abroad below the home price or below cost."],["Transfer pricing","Prices charged between parts of the same company in different countries."],["Countertrade","Paying for trade with goods instead of money."]],
 mistakes:["Saying geocentric means 'the same price everywhere'. That is ethnocentric.","Defining dumping as 'any low price'.","Forgetting exchange rates and price escalation.","Not saying how to stop grey-market goods."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Korean electric scooter brand sells in Vietnam, the Philippines and Australia. It charges the Korean price plus shipping and import duty in every country. Sales are strong in Australia but very slow in Vietnam and the Philippines. Name this pricing approach, explain why it is failing, and compare two other approaches the brand could use.",
  plan:["Name the approach: ethnocentric (extension) pricing.","Why it fails: ignores local incomes and competitors' prices.","Option 1: polycentric (adaptation) pricing, with its risk.","Option 2: geocentric pricing, with its benefit.","Recommend one, with a reason."],
  model:[["Approach","Charging the home price plus shipping and duty everywhere is ethnocentric, or extension, pricing."],["Why it fails","It ignores local conditions. Incomes in Vietnam and the Philippines are much lower than in Australia, and local scooter brands are cheaper, so the price looks far too high there."],["Polycentric option","Each country could set its own price. This fits local incomes, but if prices differ a lot, traders may move scooters from a cheap country to a dear one and sell them without warranty."],["Geocentric option","Head office sets a pricing policy, and each country adjusts the actual price for its incomes, costs and competition, within limits. The brand image stays consistent and price gaps stay manageable."],["Recommend","Geocentric pricing is the best fit, because the brand needs lower prices in two countries without losing control of its image."]]},
 check:[["What is the difference between dumping and a normal low price?","Dumping is below the home price or below cost; a normal low price comes from lower costs and still covers them."],["Under which Incoterm does the seller pay everything, including import duty?","DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)."],["What is transfer pricing?","Prices one part of a company charges another part in a different country."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain ethnocentric, polycentric and geocentric pricing in simple English, using one brand selling in three Asian countries.","Explain grey markets and five ways a brand can reduce them. Then give me a short case and ask me to recommend two.","Quiz me on Incoterms, dumping, transfer pricing and countertrade with five short questions. Correct my answers."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 9 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[9]={wk:9,title:"Global Marketing Channels and Physical Distribution",
 big:"A great product fails abroad if it cannot reach the right shelf, at the right time, in the right condition, at the right cost.",
 sum:"Consumer and industrial channel structures, guidelines for working with intermediaries, retail formats around the world, and the six transport modes.",
 min:[
  "A channel is the route from the maker to the end buyer. Consumer channels can be direct or go through wholesalers and retailers. Industrial channels are often shorter.",
  "Choose intermediaries carefully: research the market, choose partners with good coverage and reputation, train and motivate them, and control performance.",
  "Retail formats differ by country: from small family shops to hypermarkets, convenience chains and online stores.",
  "Physical distribution covers order processing, warehousing, stock and transport. Choosing the transport mode is a trade-off between speed, cost and reliability.",
  "The six transport modes: rail, truck, air, water, pipeline and internet (for digital goods)."
 ],
 lo:"Compare channel structures, explain guidelines for working with intermediaries, describe retail formats around the world, and compare transport modes.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Channel structures",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Consumer and industrial channels",cols:[["Consumer channels",["Direct: maker to consumer (online, own stores)","Maker → retailer → consumer","Maker → wholesaler → retailer → consumer","Maker → agent → wholesaler → retailer → consumer"]],["Industrial channels",["Direct: maker's sales force to business buyer","Maker → distributor → business buyer","Maker → agent → business buyer"]]]},
   l:[["Consumer channels","Six main options, from selling direct (online, mail order, door-to-door, the maker's own stores or sales force) to longer chains through agents, wholesalers and retailers."],["Industrial (business) channels","Built from three parts: the maker's own sales force, distributors or agents, and wholesalers. The maker can sell direct, through wholesalers, or both."],["Channel length","How many intermediaries are between maker and buyer."],["Channel width","How many outlets carry the product: intensive, selective or exclusive distribution."]],
   eg:["Example: Shopee and Lazada let foreign brands sell almost directly to consumers in Southeast Asia.","Example: A medical equipment maker sells hospital machines through its own sales engineers (direct industrial channel).","Example: In the Maldives, many imported foods go from an importer-wholesaler in Malé to small shops across the atolls."]},
  {h:"Guidelines for working with intermediaries",dia:{type:"flow",title:"Building a channel abroad",steps:[["Research the market","Who are the main distributors? How do buyers shop?"],["Choose partners","Coverage, reputation, finance, fit with your brand."],["Agree terms","Territory, targets, pricing, exclusivity, length of contract."],["Support and motivate","Training, marketing support, fair margins."],["Control and review","Measure sales and service; replace weak partners."]]},
   tip:"In many countries, contracts with distributors are hard to end. Use a trial period and clear targets."},
  {h:"Retail formats around the world",l:[["Department stores","Many product lines in one large store."],["Specialty stores","Narrow range with deep choice (sports, electronics)."],["Supermarkets","Self-service food and household goods."],["Convenience stores","Small, open long hours, near homes and transport; impulse buys."],["Discount stores","Low prices, basic service."],["Hypermarkets","Very large stores mixing a supermarket and a discount store."],["Category killers / superstores","Huge choice in one category at low prices."],["Outlet stores and malls","Brands sell past-season or surplus stock at lower prices."],["Online and social commerce","Marketplaces, brand sites and selling through social media and live streams."]],
   eg:["Example: In India, small family shops (kirana stores) still sell most groceries, so brands use many small wholesalers.","Example: In Thailand and Japan, convenience-store chains are a major channel for snacks and drinks.","Example: Live-stream shopping on TikTok Shop is now an important channel in Indonesia."]},
  {h:"Physical distribution and transport modes",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Six transport modes",cols:[["Rail",["Heavy goods over land","Low cost, reliable","Needs rail links"]],["Truck",["Door to door","Flexible","Costly over long distances"]],["Air",["Fastest","Most expensive","For valuable, light or perishable goods"]],["Water (sea)",["Cheapest for large loads","Slow; weather delays","Most world trade"]],["Pipeline",["Oil, gas, water","Very reliable","Only for some products"]],["Internet",["Digital goods and services","Instant","Software, music, e-books, apps"]]]},
   l:[["Reliability","How often goods arrive on time."],["Accessibility","Whether the mode can reach the place."],["Cost and speed","Faster usually costs more."],["Cold chain","Temperature-controlled storage and transport for fresh, frozen or medical goods."]],
   eg:["Example: Fresh reef fish and orchids are flown from Asia to Europe because they spoil quickly.","Example: Most goods for the Maldives arrive by sea container, then travel to the atolls by smaller boats.","Example: Vaccines need an unbroken cold chain from factory to clinic."]},
  {h:"Designing a supply chain abroad",l:["Make or store goods closer to customers when speed or freshness matters.","Use a regional warehouse or hub (for example Singapore or Dubai) to serve several countries.","Balance stock: too little loses sales; too much ties up cash and can spoil.","Set quality checks at each step."],
   eg:["Example: A cosmetics brand serving the Gulf keeps stock in a Dubai free-zone warehouse so stores in five countries can be restocked within days."]}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Map the route","Maker → … → end buyer. Who is in the chain?"],["Check the product","Fresh, fragile, valuable, heavy or digital?"],["Choose channels and partners","Use the guidelines; say why this partner."],["Choose transport","Compare speed, cost, reliability, access."],["Add control","Quality checks, stock levels, partner targets."]]},
 terms:[["Marketing channel","The route a product takes from maker to end buyer."],["Intermediary","A firm between maker and buyer, such as an agent, distributor, wholesaler or retailer."],["Channel length","The number of intermediaries in a channel."],["Exclusive distribution","Only one or a few outlets in an area carry the product."],["Hypermarket","A very large store combining a supermarket and a discount store."],["Convenience store","A small store with long hours, near customers, for quick purchases."],["Physical distribution","Moving and storing goods: orders, warehousing, stock and transport."],["Cold chain","Temperature-controlled transport and storage."],["Regional hub","A central location that serves several nearby countries."]],
 mistakes:["Listing transport modes without comparing speed, cost and reliability.","Ignoring the product's needs (fresh, fragile, heavy).","Choosing a partner without saying why.","Forgetting that retail formats differ by country."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Sri Lankan cut-flower exporter wants to supply hotels and florists in the UAE. Recommend a channel and a transport mode, and explain one way to keep quality high.",
  plan:["Channel: importer-distributor in Dubai who supplies hotels and florists.","Why: knows buyers, has cold storage, handles customs.","Transport: air freight, because flowers spoil fast.","Quality: cold chain from farm to hotel; checks on arrival.","Conclusion."],
  model:[["Channel","The exporter should sell through a specialist flower importer in Dubai that already supplies hotels and florists. It knows the buyers, has cold rooms and handles customs."],["Transport","Air freight is the only sensible mode: flowers lose quality within days, and the flight from Colombo to Dubai is short. Sea freight is too slow."],["Quality","Use a cold chain: cool the flowers at the farm, ship them in temperature-controlled packaging, and check temperature and freshness on arrival. Agree quality standards in the contract."],["Conclusion","A specialist partner plus air freight and a cold chain lets the exporter deliver fresh flowers reliably at a price hotels accept."]]},
 check:[["Which transport mode is fastest but most expensive?","Air."],["What is a hypermarket?","A very large store combining a supermarket and a discount store."],["Give two guidelines for choosing an overseas distributor.","Any two: research the market, check coverage and reputation, agree clear terms, support and train, control performance."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Compare the six transport modes in simple English, and give me an Asian example of a product suited to each.","Give me three products and markets. Ask me to design a channel and choose a transport mode for each. Mark my answers.","Explain how retail formats differ between India, Japan and the Gulf states, in simple English."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 12 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[12]={wk:12,title:"Global Marketing and the Digital Revolution",
 big:"Digital technology lets any brand reach customers in many countries at once, but the website, app and payment must still feel local.",
 sum:"The digital revolution, convergence, value networks, sustaining and disruptive technologies, global e-commerce trends, designing websites for different countries, and new products of the last decade.",
 min:[
  "The digital revolution came from a chain of inventions: computers, the internet, the web, mobile phones and cloud computing.",
  "Convergence is when industries, products and technologies that used to be separate come together because of digital technology.",
  "Disruptive technologies start simple and cheap, serve customers others ignore, then improve and overturn established firms.",
  "E-commerce keeps growing, led by mobile shopping, marketplaces, social commerce and cross-border buying.",
  "A global website needs local language, currency, payment methods, delivery options, legal rules and culturally suitable images."
 ],
 lo:"Explain convergence and disruptive technology with examples, describe e-commerce trends, and evaluate the issues in designing a website for customers in different countries.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"The digital revolution",l:["Digitisation: turning words, sounds and pictures into binary code (bits) that computers can store and send.","The transistor, then the integrated circuit (microchip), made electronics small, cheap and reliable, which led to the personal computer (PC).","The internet and the World Wide Web linked people and businesses around the world.","Mobile phones, then smartphones and mobile internet, put the web in people's pockets.","Cloud computing, social media, big data and artificial intelligence changed how firms reach and serve customers."],
   eg:["Example: In the Maldives, most people now shop, bank and book ferries through mobile apps."]},
  {h:"Convergence",p:"Convergence means industries, products and technologies that used to be separate come together, because everything can now be digital.",
   l:[["Industry convergence","Telecom, banking, media and retail now compete in the same space."],["Product convergence","One device or app does the jobs of many separate products."]],
   eg:["Example: Super apps such as Grab and Gojek combine transport, food delivery, payments and loans in one app.","Example: Mobile money services let phone companies act like banks: customers pay bills and send money from their phones.","Example: Modern cars are becoming computers on wheels, linking maps, music, calls and software updates."],
   tip:"After defining convergence, say how it changes the way brands reach customers: one platform, many countries, direct contact, targeted messages."},
  {h:"Value networks and disruptive technology",dia:{type:"compare",title:"Sustaining or disruptive?",cols:[["Sustaining technology",["Improves existing products for current customers","Established firms usually lead","Example: a better camera on each new phone model"]],["Disruptive technology",["Starts simpler and cheaper","Serves ignored or new customers","Improves until it overturns established firms","Example: digital cameras replacing film"]]]},
   l:[["Value network","The set of customers, suppliers and partners around a firm that shapes what it sees as valuable."]],
   eg:["Example: Kodak invented an early digital camera but stayed focused on film, its existing value network. Digital photography later destroyed its film business.","Example: Ride-hailing apps disrupted traditional taxi firms in many Asian cities."]},
  {h:"Global e-commerce trends",l:[["Mobile commerce","Most online shopping in Asia happens on phones."],["Marketplaces","Amazon, Alibaba, Shopee and Lazada give brands instant reach."],["Social commerce","Buying through social media and live streams."],["Cross-border e-commerce","Buying directly from sellers in other countries."],["Digital payments","E-wallets, QR codes and buy-now-pay-later."]],
   eg:["Example: Alibaba's Singles' Day (11 November) is the world's biggest online shopping event.","Example: Small Maldivian businesses sell clothes and food through Instagram and accept bank transfers and card payments."]},
  {h:"Designing a website for different countries",l:[["Language","Professional translation, not just automatic; right-to-left layout for Arabic and Dhivehi."],["Local currency and prices","Show prices in the visitor's currency, including taxes."],["Payment methods","Cards in some countries; e-wallets, bank transfer or cash on delivery in others."],["Delivery and returns","Local delivery times, costs and return rules."],["Culture and images","Colours, models and symbols that suit local values."],["Legal rules","Privacy and data laws, consumer rights, cookie notices."],["Speed and devices","Fast loading on mobile networks; mobile-first design."],["Domain and search","Country domains or folders; local search engines and keywords."]],
   eg:["Example: Booking.com shows hotels in dozens of languages and currencies and suggests local payment options.","Example: Uniqlo runs country websites with local sizes, prices and delivery options."],
   tip:"For a design question, pick three issues, explain each, and give a specific example of what the site should do."},
  {h:"New products and services of the last decade",l:["Smartphones with powerful cameras and mobile payments.","Streaming music and video services.","Smart speakers and voice assistants.","Electric vehicles and ride-hailing.","Wearables such as smartwatches.","Generative artificial intelligence tools."]}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Spot the digital change","Convergence, disruption, e-commerce, social media."],["Explain the effect","How does it change the way the brand reaches or serves customers?"],["Localise the digital offer","Language, currency, payment, delivery, law."],["Give an example","What the site, app or campaign would do in one country."],["Note a risk","Data privacy, fake reviews, competition from global platforms."]]},
 terms:[["Convergence","Industries, products and technologies coming together because of digital technology."],["Value network","The customers, suppliers and partners that shape what a firm values."],["Sustaining technology","An improvement to existing products for current customers."],["Disruptive technology","A simpler, cheaper innovation that later overturns established firms."],["Mobile commerce","Buying and selling through mobile devices."],["Social commerce","Buying through social media platforms."],["Cross-border e-commerce","Online buying from sellers in other countries."],["Localisation (web)","Adapting a website's language, currency, payments, images and rules for each country."]],
 mistakes:["Defining convergence only as 'new technology'.","Calling every new product 'disruptive'.","For website design, only saying 'translate the website'.","Giving a digital idea without a country example."],
 worked:{marks:6,q:"A Maldivian guesthouse booking platform wants to attract travellers from China, Germany and Saudi Arabia. Many visitors from these countries leave the site without booking. Suggest how to change the website for each group, and explain why.",
  plan:["Issue 1: language and layout (Chinese, German, Arabic right-to-left).","Issue 2: payment methods (Alipay/WeChat Pay; cards/PayPal; local cards).","Issue 3: culture and content (family and halal information; images).","Optional: privacy law (GDPR for Germany).","Example for each."],
  model:[["Language","The site needs proper Chinese, German and Arabic versions, written by native speakers. The Arabic version should read right to left."],["Payment","Chinese travellers often pay with Alipay or WeChat Pay; German travellers use cards or bank transfer; Saudi travellers use local cards. Offering these increases bookings."],["Culture and content","Saudi families may want to see halal food, prayer facilities and family rooms clearly. German visitors may want detailed information on sustainability and diving."],["Law","Because the site collects data from German users, it must follow the EU's privacy rules (GDPR)."]]},
 check:[["Give one example of convergence.","Any one: super apps combining transport, food and payments; mobile money; cars as connected computers."],["What is a disruptive technology?","A simpler, cheaper innovation that serves ignored customers, then improves and overturns established firms."],["Name three issues in designing a website for different countries.","Any three: language, currency, payment, delivery, culture and images, law, speed, domain and search."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain convergence and disruptive technology in simple English, with Asian examples, and quiz me.","Give me a business and three countries. Ask me to list how its website should change for each country. Correct my answer.","Explain the main global e-commerce trends in Asia in simple English, with examples."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 13 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[13]={wk:13,title:"Strategic Elements of Competitive Advantage",
 big:"To win abroad, understand the forces in the industry, build an advantage rivals cannot easily copy, and where possible create a market with no direct rivals.",
 sum:"Porter's five forces, competitive advantage and generic strategies, the national diamond, hypercompetition, strategic intent and Blue Ocean Strategy.",
 min:[
  "Competitive advantage means offering customers more value than rivals, in a way that lasts.",
  "Porter's five forces explain how attractive an industry is: rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power and supplier power.",
  "Generic strategies: cost leadership, differentiation, and focus (cost focus or differentiation focus).",
  "Porter's diamond explains why some nations are strong in some industries: factor conditions, demand conditions, related and supporting industries, and firm strategy, structure and rivalry, plus government and chance.",
  "In hypercompetitive industries, advantages do not last, so firms keep moving.",
  "Strategic intent is a bold long-term goal that drives a firm to build new strengths. Blue Ocean Strategy creates new market space using Eliminate–Reduce–Raise–Create."
 ],
 lo:"Apply the five forces with examples, explain competitive advantage and the national diamond, and evaluate how strategic intent and Blue Ocean Strategy help a firm compete with bigger rivals.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Porter's five forces",dia:{type:"flow",title:"Five forces that shape competition",steps:[["Rivalry among existing competitors","How fierce is competition now?"],["Threat of new entrants","How easy is it for new firms to enter?"],["Threat of substitutes","Can customers meet the same need another way?"],["Bargaining power of buyers","Can customers push prices down or switch easily?"],["Bargaining power of suppliers","Can suppliers push costs up?"]]},
   eg:["Example (rivalry): Budget airlines in Southeast Asia compete hard on price on the same routes.","Example (new entrants): Opening an online clothing shop is easy, so the threat of new entrants is high.","Example (substitutes): Video calls are a substitute for some business flights.","Example (buyer power): Big supermarket chains can demand low prices from snack makers.","Example (supplier power): A few chip makers supply most advanced processors, so phone makers have little power over them."],
   tip:"For each force, name it, say whether it is high or low, and give the case fact that shows it."},
  {h:"Competitive advantage and generic strategies",dia:{type:"grid",title:"Porter's generic strategies",yl:"Scope",y:["Broad","Narrow"],xl:"Source of advantage",x:["Lower cost","Differentiation"],cells:[["Cost leadership","Lowest cost in a broad market."],["Differentiation","Unique value in a broad market."],["Cost focus","Lowest cost in a niche."],["Differentiation focus","Unique value in a niche."]]},
   eg:["Example (cost leadership): AirAsia keeps costs low with one aircraft type and online booking.","Example (differentiation): Dyson charges premium prices for distinctive design and technology.","Example (differentiation focus): A small Maldivian liveaboard company offers expert-led diving trips for serious divers only."]},
  {h:"Porter's diamond: national competitive advantage",dia:{type:"compare",title:"The national diamond",cols:[["Factor conditions",["Natural resources, skilled labour, infrastructure, capital, knowledge"]],["Demand conditions",["Demanding home customers push firms to improve"]],["Related and supporting industries",["Strong local suppliers and linked industries (clusters)"]],["Firm strategy, structure and rivalry",["Strong local competition drives innovation"]]]},
   more:"Government and chance also affect the diamond: policies, education, trade deals, wars or new inventions.",
   eg:["Example: Japan's car industry: demanding home buyers who want reliable, efficient cars (demand); a large network of parts suppliers (related industries); fierce rivalry between Toyota, Honda, Nissan and others (rivalry).","Example: Sri Lanka's tea industry: the right climate and highland soil and generations of skilled pickers (factor conditions), plus tea research, auction and packing businesses (related industries).","Example: Italy's fashion and ceramic tile industries grew from clusters of skilled firms and suppliers in the same regions."],
   tip:"Apply each determinant to the country and industry in the case, then give a judgement: does the nation give an advantage, and is it weaker for some markets?"},
  {h:"Hypercompetition",p:"In hypercompetitive industries, firms move so fast that any advantage is short-lived. Competition happens in four arenas.",
   l:[["Cost and quality","Firms keep raising quality and cutting price."],["Timing and know-how","Being first, or copying fast."],["Strongholds (entry barriers)","Building protected home markets, then attacking rivals' strongholds in other countries."],["Deep pockets","Firms with more money can outlast rivals."]],
   eg:["Example: Smartphone makers launch new models every year and copy each other's features within months."]},
  {h:"Strategic intent",p:"Strategic intent is a bold, long-term goal that guides the whole company. Instead of copying bigger rivals, the firm builds new core competencies and changes the rules of the game.",
   l:[["Layers of advantage","Build several strengths on top of each other (for example low cost, then quality, then brand), so one rival move cannot beat you."],["Loose bricks","Attack the gaps a leader ignores: a segment, a product size or a country it does not defend."],["Changing the rules","Refuse to compete the leader's way; sell, price or deliver differently."],["Collaborating","Learn and gain know-how through licensing, partnerships and alliances."]],
   more:"The textbook gives these four ways firms with strategic intent beat bigger rivals.",
   eg:["Example: Komatsu's goal 'Encircle Caterpillar' guided it from a small Japanese firm to a global rival of the world leader in earth-moving machines.","Example: Canon challenged Xerox with small, cheap copiers sold through dealers to small offices, which Xerox ignored."]},
  {h:"Blue Ocean Strategy and ERRC",p:"Red oceans are crowded markets where rivals fight over the same customers. A blue ocean is new market space where competition does not matter yet. The ERRC grid helps create one.",
   dia:{type:"grid",title:"The ERRC grid",yl:"",y:["",""],xl:"",x:["",""],cells:[["Eliminate","Which factors the industry takes for granted can be dropped?"],["Raise","Which factors should be raised well above the industry standard?"],["Reduce","Which factors can be reduced below the standard?"],["Create","Which new factors, never offered before, should be created?"]]},
   eg:["Example: Cirque du Soleil eliminated animal acts and star performers, reduced thrill and danger, raised artistic quality and a unique venue, and created a theatre-style story. It drew adults who never went to circuses.","Example: Nintendo's Wii eliminated the race for the most powerful graphics, reduced complexity, and created motion controls that brought families and older people into gaming."],
   tip:"Use all four ERRC boxes with specific examples for the company. Then say why rivals would find it hard to copy: skills, culture, relationships, location or assets they do not have."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Analyse the industry","Pick the two or three strongest forces; use case facts."],["Check the home base","Use the diamond: does the home country give an advantage?"],["Set the strategic intent","A bold, long-term goal that fits the firm's strengths."],["Design the blue ocean","Fill in Eliminate, Reduce, Raise, Create."],["Recommend one move","Say why it fits, and why rivals cannot copy it easily."]]},
 terms:[["Competitive advantage","Offering more value than rivals in a lasting way."],["Five forces","Rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power and supplier power."],["Cost leadership","Competing by having the lowest costs."],["Differentiation","Competing by offering unique value."],["Focus strategy","Serving a narrow segment with low cost or unique value."],["National diamond","Porter's model of why nations succeed in certain industries."],["Cluster","A group of linked firms and suppliers in one region."],["Hypercompetition","Fast, intense competition where advantages do not last."],["Strategic intent","A bold long-term goal that drives a firm to build new competencies."],["Blue Ocean Strategy","Creating new market space where competition is irrelevant."],["ERRC grid","Eliminate, Reduce, Raise, Create: a tool for designing a blue ocean."]],
 mistakes:["Naming a force without applying it to the case.","Listing all diamond determinants without a judgement.","Using ERRC with vague words ('raise quality') and no specifics.","Recommending a move that big rivals could copy tomorrow."],
 worked:{marks:10,q:"A Malaysian e-wallet company wants to expand into Indonesia, where large local e-wallets already have millions of users. Use two of Porter's five forces to analyse the market, then use the ERRC grid to suggest how the company could create a blue ocean.",
  plan:["Force 1: rivalry (strong local e-wallets, cashback wars).","Force 2: buyer power (users switch easily; many apps free).","State strategic intent briefly.","ERRC: eliminate, reduce, raise, create with specifics.","Why hard to copy; short conclusion."],
  model:[["Rivalry","Rivalry is very high. Large local e-wallets already have millions of users and spend heavily on cashback, so a newcomer cannot win by copying them."],["Buyer power","Buyer power is high. Users can download several free apps and switch for the best cashback, so loyalty is low."],["Strategic intent","Aim to become the leading e-wallet for cross-border Malaysia–Indonesia payments within five years, instead of fighting the giants head-on."],["ERRC","Eliminate: costly general cashback campaigns. Reduce: the number of everyday features. Raise: speed and low fees for sending money between Malaysia and Indonesia. Create: a service for Indonesian workers in Malaysia and their families at home, with instant transfers and bill payment in both countries."],["Hard to copy","The company already has a licence, partners and users in Malaysia, which Indonesian rivals lack. This cross-border network takes years to build."]]},
 check:[["Name Porter's five forces.","Rivalry, threat of new entrants, threat of substitutes, buyer power, supplier power."],["What do the letters ERRC stand for?","Eliminate, Reduce, Raise, Create."],["Name the four main determinants of Porter's diamond.","Factor conditions, demand conditions, related and supporting industries, firm strategy, structure and rivalry."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain Porter's five forces in simple English with an Asian example for each, then give me a short industry case to analyse.","Explain Porter's diamond using Japan's car industry, then ask me to apply it to another country and industry. Mark my answer.","Explain Blue Ocean Strategy and the ERRC grid with two famous examples, then ask me to build an ERRC grid for a new business."]
};

/* ===================== WEEK 14 ===================== */
NOTES301.weeks[14]={wk:14,title:"Leadership, Organisation and Corporate Social Responsibility",
 big:"Global growth needs leaders with a clear vision, a structure that fits the business, and responsible behaviour that protects the brand.",
 sum:"The role of global leaders, organisation structures for global firms, lean production, building an international marketing plan, and corporate social responsibility (including Starbucks and Global Exchange).",
 min:[
  "Top managers set the global vision and strategy, build commitment across countries, use talent from everywhere, and grow core competencies.",
  "As firms grow abroad, their structure changes: export department, international division, regional or geographic structure, global product divisions, matrix, and transnational.",
  "Lean production cuts waste and keeps quality high; Toyota pioneered it.",
  "An international marketing plan brings every topic together: company strengths, the market environment, STP, entry strategy, the marketing mix, organisation and control, and future challenges.",
  "Corporate social responsibility (CSR) means acting in the interest of people, society and the environment, not only shareholders. Poor CSR can damage a global brand fast."
 ],
 lo:"Assess the role of global leaders, compare organisation structures, explain lean production, build an international marketing plan, and evaluate CSR lessons for global marketers.",
 ideas:[
  {h:"Leadership in global marketing",l:[["Vision and strategy","Leaders set where the company is going and how it will compete worldwide."],["Organisational commitment","They build a shared culture so staff in every country pull in the same direction."],["Global talent","They use the best people from any country and build diverse teams."],["Core competencies","They protect and grow what the company does best, such as design, logistics or service."]],
   eg:["Example: Under Satya Nadella, Microsoft moved its whole global strategy towards cloud services and built a more open, learning culture.","Example: A Maldivian company expanding abroad needs its founders to agree one vision before choosing markets."]},
  {h:"Organisation structures for global firms",dia:{type:"flow",title:"How structure changes as a firm grows abroad",steps:[["Export department","A small team at home handles foreign sales."],["International division","One division runs all business outside the home country."],["Regional management centre","A regional head office (for example for Asia-Pacific or the Middle East) that manages countries with similar conditions."],["Geographic / regional structure","Managers run regions such as Asia-Pacific or the Middle East."],["Global product divisions","Each product line is run worldwide."],["Matrix structure","Managers report to both a region and a product line."],["Transnational","A network: decisions made where the skills are; shares learning across countries."]]},

   eg:["Example: Unilever organises its business into global product groups (such as beauty and food) that work with country teams.","Example: A growing Asian fashion brand may start with an international division, then move to regional managers for the Gulf and Southeast Asia."],
   tip:"Recommend a structure by matching it to the firm's size, number of countries, and whether products or regions differ more."},
  {h:"Lean production",p:"Lean production makes products with less waste: less stock, less waiting, fewer defects, and constant improvement.",
   l:[["Just-in-time","Parts arrive only when needed, so little stock is held."],["Kaizen","Continuous small improvements by every worker."],["Quality at the source","Workers can stop the line to fix problems immediately."],["Teamwork and supplier links","Close long-term relationships with suppliers."]],
   eg:["Example: Toyota pioneered lean production with the Toyota Production System; many firms worldwide now copy it.","Example: Hospitals and airlines also use lean ideas to cut waiting times."]},
  {h:"Building an international marketing plan",p:"Your group marketing plan, and many practice cases, need a full plan for entering a foreign market. Use a clear order.",
   dia:{type:"flow",title:"International marketing plan",steps:[["Company strengths and limits","What gives the firm an advantage abroad, and what holds it back?"],["Market environment","The most important economic, cultural, political and legal points for this market."],["Segmentation, targeting and positioning","Who to serve and how to stand out."],["Entry strategy","Compare two or three entry modes and choose one, with reasons."],["Marketing mix","Product, price, promotion and distribution for this market."],["Organisation, budget and control","Who runs it, what it costs, and how progress is checked."],["Challenges ahead","Environmental changes in the next 3 to 5 years and what they mean."]]},
   l:[["Ways to keep control","Brand and operations manuals, training, audits, a pilot market first, staged investment, contracts, hedging currency."]],
   tip:"Connect every decision to a fact about the company or the market. Plans that ignore the firm's limits (money, people, distance) are weak."},
  {h:"Corporate social responsibility",p:"CSR means a company acts in the interests of employees, customers, communities and the environment, as well as shareholders. In global marketing, CSR failures in one country can harm the brand everywhere.",
   l:[["Ethical sourcing","Fair pay and safe conditions for suppliers' workers."],["Environment","Cutting waste, emissions and plastic."],["Community","Supporting local people where the firm operates."],["Transparency","Being honest about how products are made."]],
   eg:["Example: Global Exchange, a human-rights group, pressed Starbucks to sell Fair Trade coffee. CEO Howard Schultz could ignore it, fight back or give in. He chose a middle way: Fair Trade coffee in Starbucks' own US stores, plus long-term direct links with coffee growers. The textbook's lessons: work with NGOs before a crisis, think strategically about these relationships, accept some compromise, value the NGO's independence, and communicate clearly.","Example: Many Maldivian resorts run reef restoration and local hiring programmes, which also support their brand with eco-conscious guests."],
   tip:"For a CSR question, give the issue, what the company did, and the lesson for other global marketers."}
 ],
 apply:{h:"How to use this on a case",steps:[["Check leadership","Do the leaders share one vision? What competencies must they protect?"],["Choose a structure","Fit size, number of countries and product variety."],["Build the plan","Strengths, environment, STP, entry, marketing mix, control."],["Name the biggest risk","And a practical control."],["Add CSR","One responsible action that also helps the brand."]]},
 terms:[["Core competence","Something a firm does especially well that gives it an advantage."],["International division","A division that manages all business outside the home country."],["Geographic structure","An organisation built around regions."],["Global product division","A structure where each product line is managed worldwide."],["Matrix structure","Managers report to both a product line and a region."],["Transnational","A networked structure that shares skills and decisions across countries."],["Lean production","Producing with minimum waste and continuous improvement."],["Kaizen","Continuous small improvements."],["Corporate social responsibility","Acting in the interests of society and the environment as well as shareholders."]],
 mistakes:["Listing structures without saying which fits the case.","Writing a marketing plan that ignores the company's money, people or limits.","Describing CSR as only giving to charity.","Forgetting the lesson in the Starbucks and Global Exchange story."],
 worked:{marks:10,q:"A Thai sauce and spice company sells in 12 countries. All foreign business is run by one international division at head office in Bangkok. Sales in the Gulf states and in Europe are growing fast, but the two regions need very different products, halal rules and promotion. Recommend a better organisation structure, and explain how the company's leaders can keep staff in every country working towards one global strategy.",
  plan:["Problem: one central division is too far from two very different regions.","Options: geographic/regional structure, global product divisions, matrix.","Recommend: regional structure (Gulf, Europe, Asia) with regional managers.","Leadership: one clear vision, shared values, best talent from any country, protect core competencies.","Control: shared targets, regular reviews, sharing ideas between regions."],
  model:[["Problem","An international division in Bangkok worked when foreign sales were small. Now the Gulf and Europe are large and need different products, halal certification and promotion, so decisions from head office are too slow and too far from customers."],["Options","A global product structure suits firms whose products differ more than their markets. Here the markets differ more than the products (sauces and spices), so a product structure fits less well. A matrix is powerful but complex for a mid-sized firm."],["Recommendation","The company should move to a geographic (regional) structure, with managers for the Gulf, Europe and Asia. Each region adapts recipes, labels and promotion, while head office keeps the brand, core recipes and quality standards."],["Leadership","Top managers should set one clear vision (for example, to be the most trusted Thai flavour brand worldwide), build shared values through training and visits, hire the best people from any country for regional roles, and protect the core competence: authentic recipes and quality."],["Control","Regions share common targets and meet regularly to share ideas, so a success in one region can be used in others. This keeps the strategy global while decisions stay close to customers."]]},
 check:[["Name two organisation structures for global firms.","Any two: international division, regional management centre, geographic, global product division, matrix."],["What did Starbucks agree after the Global Exchange campaign?","To sell Fair Trade coffee in its own US stores, and to build long-term direct links with coffee growers."],["What is kaizen?","Continuous small improvements by everyone."]],
 prompts:["I am a final-year international marketing student. Explain the main organisation structures for global companies in simple English, and ask me which fits three short company cases.","Explain the Starbucks and Global Exchange CSR story in simple English and the lessons for global marketers.","Help me practise expansion plans. Give me a small Asian company and three markets, and mark my plan on market order, pace, entry modes and risk."]
};

